</history><permissions><!--&#x000a9; 2025 The Author(s). CA: A Cancer Journal for Clinicians published by Wiley Periodicals LLC on behalf of American Cancer Society.--><copyright-statement content-type="article-copyright">&#x000a9; 2025 The Author(s). CA: A Cancer Journal for Clinicians published by Wiley Periodicals LLC on behalf of American Cancer Society.</copyright-statement><license><ali:license_ref xmlns:ali="http://www.niso.org/schemas/ali/1.0/" specific-use="textmining" content-type="ccbyncndlicense">https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/</ali:license_ref><license-p>This is an open access article under the terms of the <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/">http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/</ext-link> License, which permits use and distribution in any medium, provided the original work is properly cited, the use is non&#x02010;commercial and no modifications or adaptations are made.</license-p></license></permissions><self-uri content-type="pdf" xlink:href="file:CAAC-75-630.pdf"/><abstract><title>Abstract</title><p>Reirradiation (reRT), defined as administering a course of radiation therapy to a specific area previously irradiated, is an evolving treatment strategy for locoregionally recurrent cancer that offers significant potential and poses inherent challenges. Advances in such techniques as intensity&#x02010;modulated and stereotactic body radiation therapy have improved precision, making reRT a viable option for complex scenarios previously deemed high&#x02010;risk. Nevertheless, reRT remains associated with substantial risks&#x02014;including life&#x02010;threatening side effects, functional impairments, and psychosocial effects&#x02014;which must be carefully balanced against the patient's overall health and the likelihood of achieving cancer control or palliation. Patient selection is essential to optimize outcomes while mitigating risks. Decisions should account for tumor characteristics at the time of primary diagnosis and recurrence, elapsed time since prior treatment, the possibility of delivering meaningful doses to the tumor, and the cumulative irradiation tolerance of normal tissues. Advanced imaging modalities, such as functional magnetic resonance imaging and fluorine&#x02010;18&#x02013;labeled fluorodeoxyglucose&#x02013;positron emission tomography, are important for distinguishing recurrences from treatment&#x02010;induced changes, refining treatment targets, and minimizing exposure to healthy tissue. Combined treatment with systemic regimens&#x02014;targeted therapies and immunotherapy in particular&#x02014;offers promising opportunities but requires coordination to manage side effects. Standardized guidelines, such as those from the European Society of Therapeutic Radiology and Oncology&#x02010;European Society for Research and Treatment of Cancer, are essential for improving the consistency of reporting, guiding clinical decision making, and fostering patient&#x02010;centered care. Multidisciplinary collaboration and ongoing research, particularly through clinical trials, are central to fully exploiting reRT strategies. In addition, the development of innovative techniques, such as proton therapy, would likely enable safer treatments. These efforts aim to improve the therapeutic balance of reRT, enhancing outcomes and quality of life.</p></abstract><kwd-group><kwd id="caac70016-kwd-0001">advanced imaging techniques</kwd><kwd id="caac70016-kwd-0002">decision&#x02010;making</kwd><kwd id="caac70016-kwd-0003">locoregional recurrence</kwd><kwd id="caac70016-kwd-0004">radiation oncology</kwd><kwd id="caac70016-kwd-0005">reirradiation</kwd></kwd-group><counts><fig-count count="14"/><table-count count="3"/><page-count count="37"/><word-count count="24614"/></counts><custom-meta-group><custom-meta><meta-name>source-schema-version-number</meta-name><meta-value>2.0</meta-value></custom-meta><custom-meta><meta-name>cover-date</meta-name><meta-value>November/December 2025</meta-value></custom-meta><custom-meta><meta-name>details-of-publishers-convertor</meta-name><meta-value>Converter:WILEY_ML3GV2_TO_JATSPMC version:6.6.4 mode:remove_FC converted:07.11.2025</meta-value></custom-meta></custom-meta-group></article-meta><notes><p content-type="self-citation">
<mixed-citation publication-type="journal" id="caac70016-cit-0217">
<string-name>
<surname>Beddok</surname>
<given-names>A</given-names>
</string-name>, <string-name>
<surname>Willmann</surname>
<given-names>J</given-names>
</string-name>, <string-name>
<surname>Embring</surname>
<given-names>A</given-names>
</string-name>, et al. <article-title>Reirradiation: standards, challenges, and patient&#x02010;focused strategies across tumor types</article-title>. <source>CA Cancer J Clin</source>. <year>2025</year>;<volume>75</volume>(<issue>6</issue>):<fpage>630</fpage>&#x02010;<lpage>666</lpage>. doi:<pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.3322/caac.70016</pub-id>
</mixed-citation>
</p></notes></front><body id="caac70016-body-0001"><sec id="caac70016-sec-0010"><title>GENERAL INTRODUCTION TO REIRRADIATION</title><p>Radiation therapy (RT) is a cornerstone in the treatment of a majority of cancers, whereby high&#x02010;energy radiation is administered to eradicate cancer cells while minimizing collateral damage to surrounding healthy tissues. Advancements in RT techniques have increased interest in the concept of reirradiation (reRT), the process of administering a consecutive course of RT to patients, often within or in close proximity to previously irradiated areas.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0001" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>1</sup>
</xref>
</p><p>The likelihood of locoregional recurrence after initial RT is multifactorial, determined primarily by tumor biology, the adequacy of initial treatments&#x02014;including surgical resection and systemic therapy&#x02014;and RT&#x02010;specific parameters, such as dose, target volume delineation, and plan quality.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0002" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>2</sup>
</xref> The risk of locoregional recurrence varies widely across cancer types: it is approximately 7% at 15 years for breast cancer across all tumor stages and molecular subtypes,<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0003" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>3</sup>
</xref> around 10% within 5 years for cervical cancer,<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0004" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>4</sup>
</xref> up to 12% within 5 years for locally advanced rectal cancer,<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0005" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>5</sup>
</xref> up to 14.6% within 8 years for high&#x02010;risk prostate cancer,<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0006" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>6</sup>
</xref> and up to 30% within 5 years for squamous cell carcinoma of the head and neck.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0007" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>7</sup>
</xref> For high&#x02010;grade brain tumors, recurrence rates can reach as high as 50% within 6 months, with almost all eventually recurring.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0008" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>8</sup>
</xref> These variations highlight the need for tailored treatment strategies. Surgical resection is often the preferred approach for managing localized tumor recurrences; however, it may not always be feasible or sufficient to obtain durable control of the disease.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0009" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>9</sup>
</xref> Thus reRT is often considered in many clinical scenarios, facilitated by increased knowledge of the radiobiology of tumors and healthy tissues in response to ionizing radiation and through contemporary technological advancements, such as intensity&#x02010;modulated RT (IMRT) and stereotactic RT, which enable targeted and safe application.</p><p>To improve management and ensure consistent reporting, the European Society for Radiotherapy and Oncology (ESTRO) and the European Organization for Research and Treatment of Cancer (EORTC) proposed a comprehensive definition of reRT, as illustrated in Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="caac70016-fig-0001" ref-type="fig">1</xref>.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0001" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>1</sup>
</xref> Specifically, <italic toggle="yes">type 1 reRT</italic> involves administering radiation to a site that (partially or fully) overlaps with previously irradiated volumes. This is typically needed for locoregional recurrences within the same area as the original tumor. <italic toggle="yes">Type 2 reRT</italic> refers to scenarios in which there is no geometric overlap between the initial and new irradiated volumes but there is concern that the cumulative dose can cause side effects. It is important to note that the terms <italic toggle="yes">repeat organ irradiation</italic> and <italic toggle="yes">repeat irradiation</italic> reflect clinical scenarios of repeated RT that fall outside the definition of reRT. <italic toggle="yes">Repeat organ irradiation</italic> refers to cases in which RT is directed at the same organ but a very different anatomic region, such as treating an asynchronously arising tumor in one hemisphere of the brain when the previous irradiation was in the contralateral hemisphere, without concerns that the cumulative dose will cause additional side effects. <italic toggle="yes">Repeat irradiation</italic> is defined as a new course of RT delivered to a field not previously irradiated in someone who had prior RT to a completely different part of the body, without any overlap between the two treatment areas. These distinctions underscore the importance of precise terminology in practice because reRT should refer specifically to scenarios involving additional risks of side effects caused by cumulative doses from multiple RTs.</p><fig position="float" fig-type="FIGURE" id="caac70016-fig-0001"><label>FIGURE 1</label><caption><p>Classification of reRT scenarios and related concepts. Red indicates the current target volume (tumor to treat in reRT), and green represents the previously treated volume (previously irradiated area). reRT indicates reirradiation. Figure created by Jonas Willmann using Biorender.com.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="CAAC-75-630-g014" position="anchor" id="jats-graphic-1"/></fig><p>ReRT is used with both curative and palliative intent.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0010" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>10</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="caac70016-bib-0011" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>11</sup>
</xref> In a curative setting, reRT may be delivered as the definitive treatment or adjuvant after surgical tumor resection. Systemic therapies are often added concomitantly or sequentially to enhance treatment efficacy. Definitive reRT aims to treat grossly visible cancer, particularly when surgery is not feasible, and may achieve long&#x02010;term disease control and potentially cure. Postoperative reRT is indicated when there is concern for residual disease, especially in patients with adverse prognostic factors, such as close or positive surgical margins. In palliative situations, reRT is intended primarily to alleviate symptoms such as pain, bleeding, or neurologic deficits rather than to achieve long&#x02010;term disease control or cure. This typically involves a lower dose or shorter course of RT, prioritizing rapid symptom relief while minimizing acute side effects. Combining systemic therapies with palliative reRT may improve symptom management and slow disease progression in carefully selected patients, depending on overall patient status and the broader treatment strategy.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0012" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>12</sup>
</xref>
</p><p>The risk of locoregional recurrence after reRT, represented by a third occurrence after initial treatment and its recurrence, remains significant for certain cancers, such as head and neck cancers (HNCs), with up to a 50% chance of a subsequent local recurrence and death within 2 years.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0013" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>13</sup>
</xref> In addition, reRT itself carries a risk of severe side effects; for example, rupture of the carotid artery can occur in up to 8% of HNC cases.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0014" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>14</sup>
</xref> Long&#x02010;term side effects&#x02014;ranging from physical discomfort and limitations in daily activities to aesthetic and psychological effects&#x02014;can also considerably reduce patients' quality of life. Key factors to consider before administering reRT include understanding the reasons for local recurrence (e.g., insufficient initial treatment, resistance of the cancer to RT), assessing the doses already received by healthy tissues, and thoughtful selection of patients (life expectancy and performance status (PS), existing organ dysfunction, tumor characteristics, and proximity to sensitive structures like major blood vessels or nerves; Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="caac70016-fig-0002" ref-type="fig">2</xref>). Striking a balance among these factors is critical to optimize treatment efficacy while minimizing the risk of severe side effects.</p><fig position="float" fig-type="FIGURE" id="caac70016-fig-0002"><label>FIGURE 2</label><caption><p>Comprehensive multidisciplinary evaluation for patient reRT. EBRT indicates external&#x02010;beam radiation therapy; reRT, reirradiation. Figure created by Jonas Willmann using Biorender.com.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="CAAC-75-630-g009" position="anchor" id="jats-graphic-3"/></fig><p>The objective of this review was to provide a comprehensive overview of current standards of reRT for locally recurrent cancer, including patient selection strategies, target volume definition, advancements in radiation physics, and innovative RT techniques. The existing literature was reviewed, and randomized clinical trials were prioritized. Expert guidelines, consensus statements, and retrospective studies with large patient cohorts were provided when high&#x02010;level evidence was lacking. How to apply these considerations to a range of specific cancer types is discussed in detail to illustrate the complexities of clinical decision making. The review concludes by offering a roadmap of key factors that must be taken into account for any patient for whom reRT is under discussion.</p></sec><sec id="caac70016-sec-0020"><title>CURRENT LEVEL OF EVIDENCE FOR REIRRADIATION</title><p>From 2000 to 2023, the number of studies on reRT increased substantially, reflecting the growing interest in and clinical need for this approach. However, the most of these studies are retrospective, often relying on small patient cohorts and reporting contradictory results.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0001" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>1</sup>
</xref> Randomized clinical trials are the gold standard for evidence, offering essential insights into the role of reRT in clinical practice. Nieder et&#x000a0;al. conducted a systematic review of published randomized trials on reRT, covering the period from 2000 to 2023.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0015" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>15</sup>
</xref> Table&#x000a0;<xref rid="caac70016-tbl-0001" ref-type="table">1</xref>
<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0016" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>16</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="caac70016-bib-0017" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>17</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="caac70016-bib-0018" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>18</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="caac70016-bib-0019" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>19</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="caac70016-bib-0020" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>20</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="caac70016-bib-0021" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>21</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="caac70016-bib-0022" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>22</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="caac70016-bib-0023" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>23</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="caac70016-bib-0024" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>24</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="caac70016-bib-0025" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>25</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="caac70016-bib-0026" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>26</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="caac70016-bib-0027" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>27</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="caac70016-bib-0028" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>28</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="caac70016-bib-0029" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>29</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="caac70016-bib-0030" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>30</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="caac70016-bib-0031" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>31</sup>
</xref> is adapted from the original review, integrating substantial additional data and new insights to provide complementary and refined information.</p><table-wrap position="float" id="caac70016-tbl-0001" content-type="TABLE"><label>TABLE 1</label><caption><p>Main characteristics and results of randomized controlled trials on reirradiation (2000&#x02013;2023).<xref rid="caac70016-note-0002" ref-type="table-fn">
<sup>a</sup>
</xref>
</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><col align="left" span="1"/><col align="left" span="1"/><col align="left" span="1"/><col align="left" span="1"/><col align="left" span="1"/><col align="left" span="1"/><col align="left" span="1"/><col align="left" span="1"/><col align="left" span="1"/><col align="left" span="1"/><col align="left" span="1"/><col align="left" span="1"/><thead valign="bottom"><tr style="border-bottom:solid 1px #000000"><th align="left" valign="bottom" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Reference</th><th align="left" valign="bottom" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Disease site</th><th align="left" valign="bottom" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Study type</th><th align="left" valign="bottom" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Objective</th><th align="left" valign="bottom" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Number of patients</th><th align="left" valign="bottom" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Interval between RT</th><th align="left" valign="bottom" rowspan="1" colspan="1">RT technique, dose</th><th align="left" valign="bottom" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Median follow&#x02010;up</th><th align="left" valign="bottom" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Local control</th><th align="left" valign="bottom" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Overall survival</th><th align="left" valign="bottom" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Severe late side effects (CTCAE grade &#x02265;3, including fatal cases)</th><th align="left" valign="bottom" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Key message</th></tr></thead><tbody valign="top"><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Li 2006<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0016" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>16</sup>
</xref>
</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Nasopharynx</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Single&#x02010;center, phase 1</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Dose escalation: 54 Gy&#x000a0;+&#x000a0;16 Gy (group I), 20 Gy (group II), or 24 Gy (group III)</td><td align="char" char="." valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">36</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x0003e;6 months</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">3D&#x02010;CRT, 54 Gy&#x000a0;+&#x000a0;boost (4 Gy &#x000d7; 3/week)</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">27 months (range, 14&#x02013;44 months)</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">3&#x02010;year LFFS, 45% (whole cohort); LFFS per subgroup: 72% for group III, 37% for group I, and 28% for group II (<italic toggle="yes">p</italic>&#x000a0;=&#x000a0;0.047)</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">3&#x02010;year OS, 65% (whole cohort); 82% (group III), 72% (group I), 59% (group II; NS)</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Posterior cranial nerve injury, 31%; temporal lobe injury, 8.3% (<italic toggle="yes">p</italic>&#x000a0;&#x0003e;&#x000a0;.05), one with nasopharyngeal bleeding (group III)</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Dose escalation improves local control (higher LFFS in group III) without increasing the incidence of side effects</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Tian 2014<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0017" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>17</sup>
</xref>
</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Nasopharynx</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Single&#x02010;center, phase 2</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Dose escalation: 60 Gy (group A) vs. 68 Gy (group B)</td><td align="char" char="." valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">117</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x0003e;6 months</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">IMRT, 60 Gy vs. 68 Gy (2.0&#x02013;2.2 Gy &#x000d7;5/week)</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">25 months (range, 6&#x02013;118 months)</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">5&#x02010;year LFFS, 63.7% (group A) vs. 71% (group B; <italic toggle="yes">p</italic>&#x000a0;=&#x000a0;0.41)</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">3&#x02010;year OS, 47.7% (whole cohort); 57.4% (group A) vs. 38% (group B); 5&#x02010;year OS, 37.3% (whole cohort); 44.2% (group A) vs. 30.3% (group B; <italic toggle="yes">p</italic>&#x000a0;=&#x000a0;0.06)</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Mucosal necrosis, 50.8% (group B) vs. 28.8% (group A); massive hemorrhage, 31% (group B) vs. 18.6% (group A)</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Dose escalation improves LFFS slightly but increases the incidence of severe side effects, with no significant OS benefit</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Guan 2016<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0018" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>18</sup>
</xref>
</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Nasopharynx</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Single&#x02010;center, phase 2</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Modality combination: CCR (cisplatin 30 mg/m<sup>2</sup> weekly) vs. RT alone</td><td align="char" char="." valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">69</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x0003e;6 months</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">IMRT, 60 Gy in 27 fractions for 37 days</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">35 months (range, 2&#x02013;112 months)</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">3&#x02010;year LFFS, 56.2% (CCR) vs. 62.4% (RT; <italic toggle="yes">p</italic>&#x000a0;=&#x000a0;0.848); 5&#x02010;year LFFS, 43.4% (CCR) vs. 62.4% (RT; <italic toggle="yes">p</italic>&#x000a0;=&#x000a0;0.502)</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">3&#x02010;year OS, 68.7% (CCR) vs. 42.2% (RT; <italic toggle="yes">p</italic>&#x000a0;=&#x000a0;0.016); 5&#x02010;year OS, 41.8% (CCR) vs. 27.5% (RT; <italic toggle="yes">p</italic>&#x000a0;=&#x000a0;0.049)</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Nasopharyngeal hemorrhage, 8.8% (CCR) vs. 34.3% (RT); trismus, 0.0% (CCR) vs. 14.3% (RT)</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">CCR improves OS but not LFFS, with reduced trismus and hemorrhage compared with RT alone</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Liu 2021<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0019" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>19</sup>
</xref>
</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Nasopharynx</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Three&#x02010;center, phase 3</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Modality: ENPG vs. IMRT</td><td align="char" char="." valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">200</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">27 months (ENPG), 34 months (IMRT)</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">IMRT 60&#x02013;70 Gy (2.0&#x02013;2.36 Gy/fraction, 5/week)</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">56 months (IQR, 42&#x02013;69 months)</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">3&#x02010;year LFFS, 89.8% (ENPG) vs. 78.2% (IMRT; NS); 5&#x02010;year LFFS, 77.0% (ENPG) vs. 66.6% (IMRT; NS)</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">3&#x02010;year OS, 85.8% (ENPG) vs. 68.0% (IMRT; <italic toggle="yes">p</italic>&#x000a0;=&#x000a0;0.0015)</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Pharyngeal mucositis: 5% (ENPG) vs. 26% (IMRT); death, 5% (ENPG) vs. 20% (IMRT)</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">ENPG offers improved OS and reduced the incidence of side effects compared with IMRT</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">You 2023<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0020" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>20</sup>
</xref>
</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Nasopharynx</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Three&#x02010;center, phase 3</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Fractionation: 60 Gy in 27 fractions (SF) vs. 65 Gy in 54 fractions (HF, 2/day)</td><td align="char" char="." valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">144</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x0003e;12 months</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">IMRT, 60 Gy in 27 fractions (SF) vs. 65 Gy in 54 fractions (HF, 2/day)</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">45 months (IQR, 37.3&#x02013;53.3 months)</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">3&#x02010;year LFFS, 53.7% (HF) vs. 46.8% (SF)</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">3&#x02010;year OS, 74.6% (HF) vs. 55.0% (SF; <italic toggle="yes">p</italic>&#x000a0;=&#x000a0;0.014)</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Treatment&#x02010;associated death, 7% (HF) vs. 24% (SF); severe side effects, 34% (HF) vs. 57% (SF; <italic toggle="yes">p</italic>&#x000a0;=&#x000a0;0.023)</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">HF improves OS with reduced late severe side effects compared with SF</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Janot 2008<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0021" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>21</sup>
</xref>
</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">HNSCC</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Multicentre, phase 2</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Modality: postoperative CCR (5&#x02010;FU 800 mg/m<sup>2</sup>/day&#x000a0;+&#x000a0;hydroxyurea 1.5 g/day) vs. observation (WS)</td><td align="char" char="." valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">130</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">3.5 years (range, 0.5&#x02013;30.5 years)</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">3D&#x02010;CRT, 60 Gy in 30 fractions, 5/week</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Not reported</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">3&#x02010;year LFFS, 58% (RT) vs. 20% (WS; <italic toggle="yes">p</italic>&#x000a0;&#x0003c;&#x000a0;.0001)</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">3&#x02010;year OS, 30% in both arms (NS)</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">39% (RT) vs. 10% (WS)</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Postoperative CCR improves LFFS significantly, but does not affect OS; the incidence of side effects is higher in the CCR arm</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Tortochaux 2011<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0022" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>22</sup>
</xref>
</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">HNSCC</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Multicenter, phase 3</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Modality: CCR (hydroxyurea 1.5 g/day&#x000a0;+&#x000a0;5&#x02010;FU 800 mg/m<sup>2</sup>/day) vs. ChT (methotrexate 40 mg/m<sup>2</sup> weekly)</td><td align="char" char="." valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">57</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x0003e;6 months</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">3D&#x02010;CRT, 60 Gy in 30 fractions, 5/week</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Complete, all patients died</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Not reported</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1&#x02010;year OS, 23% (CCR) vs. 22% (ChT; NS)</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">47% (CCR) vs. 25% (ChT)</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">CCR shows no significant OS improvement compared with methotrexate, with higher late side effects</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Rud&#x0017e;ianskas 2014<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0023" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>23</sup>
</xref>
</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">HNSCC</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Single&#x02010;center, phase 2</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">RT technique comparison: EBRT vs. HDR BT</td><td align="char" char="." valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">64</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Not reported</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">EBRT 50 Gy/25 fractions vs. HDR BT 30 Gy/12 fractions</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Not reported</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">2&#x02010;year LFFS, 63% (HDR BT) vs. 25% (EBRT; <italic toggle="yes">p</italic>&#x000a0;&#x0003c;0.0001)</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">2&#x02010;year OS, 67% (HDR BT) vs. 32% (EBRT; <italic toggle="yes">p</italic>&#x000a0;=&#x000a0;0.0002)</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">35.5% (EBRT) vs. 3.1% (HDR BT)</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">HDR BT significantly improves local control and OS and has less side effects compared with EBRT</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Tao 2018<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0024" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>24</sup>
</xref>
</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">HNSCC</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Single&#x02010;center, phase 2</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Fractionation: HF (60 Gy, 1.2 Gy twice daily for 5 weeks&#x000a0;+&#x000a0;cetuximab) vs. VP (60 Gy, 2 Gy/day for 11 weeks&#x000a0;+&#x000a0;5&#x02010;FU&#x000a0;+&#x000a0;hydroxyurea)</td><td align="char" char="." valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">63</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Not reported</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">HF 3D&#x02010;CRT, 60 Gy in 50 fractions (1.2 Gy twice daily, 5 weeks); VP 3D&#x02010;CRT, 60 Gy in 30 fractions (2 Gy/day, 11 weeks)</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">36 months (95% CI, 31&#x02013;43 months)</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Not reported</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">2&#x02010;year OS, 45% (VP) vs. 67% (HF; NS)</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1&#x02010;year side effects, 14% (VP) vs. 27% (HF); 2&#x02010;year side effects, 0% (VP) vs. 14% (HF)</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Accelerated fractionation increases late side effects compared with conventional fractionation</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Chow 2014<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0025" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>25</sup>
</xref>
</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Bone metastases</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Multicenter, phase 3</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Fractionation: Single vs. multiple fractions</td><td align="char" char="." valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">850</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x0003e;4 weeks</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">3D&#x02010;CRT: 8 Gy in one fraction vs. 20 Gy in five fractions</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">12.2 months (95% CI, 12.1&#x02013;12.3 months)</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Pain response (2 months), 28% (one fraction) vs. 32% (five fractions; <italic toggle="yes">p</italic>&#x000a0;=&#x000a0;0.21)</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1&#x02010;year OS, 53% (one fraction) vs. 52% (five fractions; NS)</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Not reported</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">A single 8&#x02010;Gy fraction achieves pain relief and survival outcomes comparable to multiple fractions</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Wick 2014<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0026" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>26</sup>
</xref>
</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Glioblastoma</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Multicenter, phase 2</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Modality combination: reRT (36 Gy in 2 Gy/day)&#x000a0;+&#x000a0;APG101 (400 mg weekly intravenously) vs. reRT alone</td><td align="char" char="." valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">91</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">20.3 months (reRT) vs. 23.9 months (reRT&#x000a0;+&#x000a0;APG101)</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Fractionated stereotactic RT, 36 Gy (5&#x000a0;&#x000d7;&#x000a0;2 Gy/week)</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">11.4 months</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">6&#x02010;month PFS, 3.8% (reRT) vs. 20.7% (reRT&#x000a0;+&#x000a0;APG101; <italic toggle="yes">p</italic>&#x000a0;=&#x000a0;0.048)</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Median OS, 11.5 months (reRT&#x000a0;+&#x000a0;APG101) vs. 11.5 months (reRT; NS)</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">41.7% overall: 50% (reRT), 37.9% (reRT&#x000a0;+&#x000a0;APG101; NS)</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Adding APG101 improves PFS but not OS compared with reRT alone</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Bergman 2020<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0027" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>27</sup>
</xref>
</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Glioblastoma</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Single&#x02010;center, phase 1</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Modality: reRT&#x000a0;+&#x000a0;BEV&#x02010;based chemotherapy vs. BEV alone</td><td align="char" char="." valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">35</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Not reported</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Fractionated stereotactic RT, 32 Gy (8 Gy &#x000d7;4 fractions in 2 weeks)</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Not reported</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">2&#x02010;month LFFS, 82% (reRT) vs. 27% (no reRT; <italic toggle="yes">p</italic>&#x000a0;=&#x000a0;0.002)</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Median OS, 6.6 months (overall); 7.2 months (reRT) vs. 4.8 months (no reRT; <italic toggle="yes">p</italic>&#x000a0;=&#x000a0;0.11)</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Not reported</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">reRT improves local control but not OS compared with BEV alone</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Voss 2020<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0028" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>28</sup>
</xref>
</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Glioblastoma</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Multicenter, phase 1</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Modality combination: reRT&#x000a0;+&#x000a0;SD vs. reRT&#x000a0;+&#x000a0;KD&#x02010;IF</td><td align="char" char="." valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">50</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">15 months (SD) vs. 13 months (KD&#x02010;IF)</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Technique unspecified, 20 Gy (5&#x000a0;&#x000d7;&#x000a0;4 Gy)</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Not reported</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">6&#x02010;month PFS, 16% (SD) vs. 20% (KD&#x02010;IF; <italic toggle="yes">p</italic>&#x000a0;=&#x000a0;0.7)</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Median OS, 9.5 months (SD) vs. 10.8 months (KD&#x02010;IF; <italic toggle="yes">p</italic>&#x000a0;=&#x000a0;0.9)</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Not reported</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Calorically restricted ketogenic diet does not improve PFS or OS compared with a standard diet when combined with reRT</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Tsien 2023<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0029" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>29</sup>
</xref>
</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Glioblastoma</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Multicenter, phase 2</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Modality: reRT&#x000a0;+&#x000a0;BEV (10 mg/kg intravenously every 2 weeks) vs. BEV alone</td><td align="char" char="." valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">182</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x0003e;6 months</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">3D&#x02010;CRT, IMRT, or proton therapy; 35 Gy (10&#x000a0;&#x000d7;&#x000a0;3.5 Gy)</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">12.8 months</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">6&#x02010;month PFS, 54.3% (reRT&#x000a0;+&#x000a0;BEV) vs. 29.1% (BEV; <italic toggle="yes">p</italic>&#x000a0;=&#x000a0;0.001)</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Median OS, 10.1 months (reRT&#x000a0;+&#x000a0;BEV) vs. 9.7 months (BEV; <italic toggle="yes">p</italic>&#x000a0;=&#x000a0;0.46)</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Treatment&#x02010;associated death: 5.3% (BEV) vs. 9.6% (reRT&#x000a0;+&#x000a0;BEV)</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">reRT combined with BEV significantly improves PFS but not OS compared with BEV alone, with a higher incidence of side effects in the reRT&#x000a0;+&#x000a0;BEV group</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Kouloulias 2003<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0030" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>30</sup>
</xref>
</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Breast cancer (skin metastases)</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Single&#x02010;center, phase 2</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Fractionation: reRT&#x000a0;+&#x000a0;liposomal doxorubicin (40 mg/m<sup>2</sup>/month for 6 months) with different fractionations</td><td align="char" char="." valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">30</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Not reported</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">3D&#x02010;CRT, 17&#x000a0;&#x000d7;&#x000a0;1.8 Gy (group A) vs. 8&#x000a0;&#x000d7;&#x000a0;3 Gy&#x000a0;+&#x000a0;1&#x000a0;&#x000d7;&#x000a0;4 Gy (group B)</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Not reported</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Not reported</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Not reported</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">11.1% (group B) vs. 0% (group A)</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Lower side effects were observed with conventional fractionation (group A) compared with hypofractionation (group B)</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Schouten 2022<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0031" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>31</sup>
</xref>
</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Breast cancer</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Single&#x02010;center, phase 2</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Modality combination: reRT&#x000a0;+&#x000a0;HT vs. reRT&#x000a0;+&#x000a0;HT&#x000a0;+&#x000a0;cisplatin (40 mg/m<sup>2</sup> weekly)</td><td align="char" char="." valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">49</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Not reported</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">3D&#x02010;CRT, 32 Gy (8&#x000a0;&#x000d7;&#x000a0;4 Gy, 2/week) pre&#x02010;2015; 46 Gy (23&#x000a0;&#x000d7;&#x000a0;2 Gy, 5/week) post&#x02010;2015</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Not reported</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1&#x02010;year LFFS, 81.5% (reRT&#x000a0;+&#x000a0;HT) vs. 88.1% (reRT&#x000a0;+&#x000a0;HT&#x000a0;+&#x000a0;CT; <italic toggle="yes">p</italic>&#x000a0;=&#x000a0;0.95)</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1&#x02010;year OS, 63.4% (reRT&#x000a0;+&#x000a0;HT) vs. 57.4% (reRT&#x000a0;+&#x000a0;HT&#x000a0;+&#x000a0;CT; NS)</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Treatment&#x02010;associated death: one (thoracic necrosis, reRT&#x000a0;+&#x000a0;HT)</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Adding chemotherapy to HT and reRT does not improve LFFS or OS</td></tr></tbody></table><table-wrap-foot id="caac70016-ntgp-0001"><fn id="caac70016-note-0001"><p>Abbreviations: 3D&#x02010;CRT, three&#x02010;dimensional conformal radiation therapy; 5&#x02010;FU, 5&#x02010;fluorouracil; APG101, a CD95L&#x02010;binding protein that blocks CD95&#x02010;dependent activation; BEV, bevacizumab; CCR, concomitant chemotherapy and radiation therapy; ChT, chemotherapy; CI, confidence interval; CT, computed tomography; CTCAE, Common Terminology Criteria for Adverse Events; DFS, disease&#x02010;free survival; EBRT, external&#x02010;beam radiation therapy; ENPG, endoscopic nasopharyngectomy; Gy, grays; HDR BT, high&#x02010;dose&#x02010;rate brachytherapy; HF, hyperfractionation; HNC, head and neck cancer; HNSCC, head and neck squamous cell carcinoma; HT, hyperthermia; IMRT, intensity&#x02010;modulated radiation therapy; IQR, interquartile range; KD&#x02010;IF, ketogenic diet; LFFS, local failure&#x02010;free survival; NS, not significant; OS, overall survival; PFS, progression&#x02010;free survival; reRT, reirradiation; RT, radiation therapy; SD, calorically unrestricted diet; SF, standard fractionation; VP, Vokes protocol; WS, wait&#x02010;and&#x02010;see strategy.</p></fn><fn id="caac70016-note-0002"><label>
<sup>a</sup>
</label><p>Adapted from: Nieder C, Willmann J, Andratschke NH. Prospective randomized clinical studies involving reirradiation: update of a systematic review. <italic toggle="yes">Strahlenther Onkol.</italic> 2023;199(9):787&#x02013;797. 10.1007/s00066&#x02010;023&#x02010;02118&#x02010;1.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0015" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>15</sup>
</xref>
</p></fn></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap><p>These trials primarily evaluated locoregional control, overall survival (OS), and progression&#x02010;free survival (PFS) as well as symptom relief in palliative settings compared with or in combination with other treatment modalities, such as surgery or systemic therapies. Several trials evaluated reRT as an experimental treatment excluded from one arm, whereas others included reRT in both arms, comparing various treatment strategies. Postoperative reRT combined with chemotherapy demonstrated significant improvements in locoregional control and PFS compared with observation, but late side effects were substantial, highlighting the importance of careful patient selection (Table&#x000a0;<xref rid="caac70016-tbl-0001" ref-type="table">1</xref>).</p><p>Most of these randomized clinical trials (nine of 16; 56%) focused on HNC, especially nasopharyngeal carcinoma. Dose&#x02010;escalation strategies in nasopharyngeal carcinoma were shown to improve recurrence&#x02010;free survival, with locoregional control rates of up to 72% at 3 years in high&#x02010;dose groups.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0016" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>16</sup>
</xref> However, severe mucosal necrosis was reported in up to 50.8% of patients who received high&#x02010;dose treatments compared with 28.8% in lower dose arms,<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0017" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>17</sup>
</xref> and hemorrhage rates ranged from 18.6% to 31%, representing a major cause of mortality in this setting. For non&#x02010;nasopharyngeal HNC, postoperative reRT combined with chemotherapy improved locoregional failure&#x02010;free survival to 58% at 3 years, compared with 20% using observation alone,<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0021" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>21</sup>
</xref> but late side effects were significant, with severe side effects occurring in 39% of patients who received reRT versus 10% in the observation group.</p><p>In glioblastoma, randomized clinical trials have primarily evaluated reRT in combination with systemic therapies, particularly bevacizumab, to assess improvements in OS and PFS. Combining reRT (35 grays [Gy] in 10 fractions) with bevacizumab nearly doubled the 6&#x02010;month PFS rate compared with bevacizumab alone (54.3% vs. 29.1%; <italic toggle="yes">p</italic>&#x000a0;=&#x000a0;0.001), whereas the median OS remained unchanged (10.1 vs. 9.7 months; <italic toggle="yes">p</italic>&#x000a0;=&#x000a0;0.46).<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0029" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>29</sup>
</xref> Side effects were more frequent in the reRT arm, with treatment&#x02010;associated death occurring in 9.6% of patients compared with 5.3% in the bevacizumab&#x02010;only group. Other trials investigating combinations with systemic agents have also reported modest PFS improvements without significant OS gains, emphasizing the need for innovative strategies to enhance survival outcomes.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0026" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>26</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="caac70016-bib-0027" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>27</sup>
</xref>
</p><p>In breast cancer, evidence for reRT is more limited, with trials focusing on locally recurrent disease and symptom relief. In one trial, reRT combined with hyperthermia achieved a 1&#x02010;year locoregional failure&#x02010;free survival rate of 88.1% versus 81.5% when cisplatin was added (<italic toggle="yes">p</italic>&#x000a0;=&#x000a0;0.95), with no significant difference in OS (57.4% vs. 63.4%).<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0031" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>31</sup>
</xref> Side effects remained high in both groups, suggesting that hyperthermia does not offer additional benefits in this setting. Nevertheless, evidence supporting the use of reRT in the framework of second breast&#x02010;conserving therapy has been rapidly accumulating.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0032" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>32</sup>
</xref>
</p><p>In the context of palliative care for bone metastases, a phase 3 trial evaluated reRT for pain relief, comparing single&#x02010;fraction reRT (8 Gy) with multiple fractions (20 Gy in five fractions). Pain relief at 2 months was comparable between the two approaches (28% vs. 32%, respectively; <italic toggle="yes">p</italic>&#x000a0;=&#x000a0;0.21).<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0025" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>25</sup>
</xref> These results established single&#x02010;fraction reRT as a practical, effective, and tolerable option for palliative management of bone metastases, particularly for patients with limited PS.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0033" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>33</sup>
</xref>
</p><p>For other cancers, randomized data on reRT are lacking. Guidelines for reRT are often based on expert consensus and limited clinical evidence, emphasizing the need for individualized decision making based on patient PS, tumor characteristics, and treatment history. Moreover, it is important to note that randomized evidence on the optimal selection and combination of treatment modalities, such as surgery, systemic therapy, and reRT, is often tumor&#x02010;specific and site&#x02010;dependent. Findings from one tumor type or anatomic site cannot be generalized to others, highlighting the need for further prospective evidence from such sources as registries, single&#x02010;arm phase 1/2 studies, or randomized clinical trials tailored to specific clinical contexts.</p><p>Data from Willmann et&#x000a0;al. (2024), who conducted a systematic review of prospective trial registrations, indicate that numerous ongoing studies are currently evaluating reRT with a focus on central nervous system cancers, HNCs, and pelvic cancers.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0034" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>34</sup>
</xref> Among 99 ongoing trials, 16 are randomized, including phase 3 trials for glioblastoma, rectal cancer, and nasopharyngeal cancer. Dose&#x02010;escalation trials combining reRT with systemic therapies or precision RT techniques (e.g., particle therapy, stereotactic RT) represent emerging areas of research. These trials are expected to address critical gaps, particularly in lung, breast, and gynecological cancers, and to help optimize multimodality treatment strategies.</p></sec><sec id="caac70016-sec-0030"><title>INTEGRATION OF REIRRADIATION INTO MULTIMODAL TREATMENT STRATEGIES</title><p>Determining the optimal application of reRT within a multimodal treatment strategy, whether combined with surgery, ablative therapies, or systemic therapy, requires an individualized approach carefully discussed among a multidisciplinary team. This ensures that the expected benefits of tumor control are properly balanced against the potential side effects. Moreover, treatment decisions should account for the patient's risk profile, including potential local versus systemic disease spread. Whereas systemic therapies aim to reduce the risk of metastatic progression, reRT, whether as a primary treatment or after surgery, can achieve local control and, in some recurrent malignancies, prolong disease control.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0035" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>35</sup>
</xref> By identifying a patient's specific risks, clinicians can better select and sequence treatments that sustain quality of life while managing disease progression.</p><p>Direct randomized comparisons between reRT and other modalities, such as surgery or systemic therapy, are scarce (Table&#x000a0;<xref rid="caac70016-tbl-0001" ref-type="table">1</xref>). In one trial focusing on recurrent nasopharyngeal cancer, endoscopic surgery significantly improved OS (85.8% vs. 68% at 3 years) versus reRT, with fewer reRT&#x02010;specific side effects, including mucositis (5% vs. 26%).<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0019" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>19</sup>
</xref> No randomized studies have directly compared reRT alone versus systemic therapy as a frontline treatment. Some insights have been gained from retrospective studies. For recurrent HNC, a retrospective study compared reRT versus systemic therapy alone in patients who were ineligible for surgery.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0036" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>36</sup>
</xref> ReRT was associated with significantly improved 2&#x02010;year OS (32% vs. 11%), PFS (31% vs. 7%), and locoregional control (39% vs. 3%) compared with systemic therapy. However, reRT also resulted in higher rates of severe side effects (53% vs. 28%) and included three treatment&#x02010;related deaths. In that study, pembrolizumab&#x02010;based regimens accounted for 64% of treatments, with other combinations, such as cisplatin, docetaxel, plus 5&#x02010;fluorouracil, contributing to 20%, and carboplatin plus paclitaxel making up 16%. Similarly, in glioblastoma, a secondary analysis of the NRG Oncology/Radiation Therapy Oncology Group Trial 0525 examined OS for patients who received no treatment, reRT alone, systemic therapy alone, or both modalities after progression.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0037" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>37</sup>
</xref> Median survival was 4.8 months for patients who received no treatment, 8.2 months with reRT alone, 10.6 months with systemic therapy alone, and 12.2 months with the combination. Although systemic therapy provided better survival outcomes compared with reRT alone, the difference was not statistically significant (hazard ratio [HR], 0.74 for reRT vs. 0.42 for systemic therapy). To date, no randomized studies have directly compared reRT versus immunotherapy. Nonetheless, the combination of nivolumab plus reRT in recurrent HNC has shown encouraging efficacy compared with historical controls, highlighting the potential of integrating these modalities.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0038" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>38</sup>
</xref>
</p><p>Moreover, combining modalities can enhance the efficacy of reRT (Table&#x000a0;<xref rid="caac70016-tbl-0001" ref-type="table">1</xref>). In a large retrospective study, surgery followed by reRT demonstrated significant benefits in recurrent HNC. This multicenter study of 412 patients reported a median OS of 16.5 months and a 2&#x02010;year OS rate of 40%.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0035" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>35</sup>
</xref> Postoperative reRT yielded better outcomes, with a 2&#x02010;year OS rate of 45.2% compared with 35.5% for definitive reRT without surgery. Among surgical patients, those with no gross residual disease achieved a 2&#x02010;year OS rate of 50.1%, compared with 33.2% for those who had residual tumors (<italic toggle="yes">p</italic>&#x000a0;=&#x000a0;0.053). Surgery enables tumor debulking, allowing reRT to target residual microscopic disease and enhance local control. This approach is particularly valuable for recurrences in which reRT can effectively manage the remaining disease after surgery. However, patients who were selected for surgery in such retrospective studies were inherently different from those who received reRT alone. Surgical candidates often have better PS, fewer comorbidities, or less advanced disease, introducing significant selection bias in nonrandomized studies. For patients in whom radical surgery poses significant functional risks or is anatomically unfeasible, such as in prostate cancer, reRT alone remains a viable alternative, offering effective tumor control with a favorable side&#x02010;effect profile.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0039" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>39</sup>
</xref> The combination of reRT and systemic therapy also may enhance outcomes in patients with locally recurrent rectal cancer. In a multicenter retrospective study of 345 patients undergoing salvage surgery, the addition of induction chemotherapy before reRT significantly improved tumor response<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0040" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>40</sup>
</xref>: among 99 patients, the pathologic complete response rate was 17%, compared with only 9% in the 111 patients who received reRT alone. Achieving a pathologic complete response was associated with significantly improved OS (HR, 0.41; <italic toggle="yes">p</italic>&#x000a0;&#x0003c;&#x000a0;.001) and locoregional control (3&#x02010;year local recurrence&#x02010;free survival, 82% vs. 44%; <italic toggle="yes">p</italic>&#x000a0;&#x0003c;&#x000a0;.001). These findings suggest that combining chemotherapy with reRT may improve long&#x02010;term oncologic outcomes. The value of this strategy is currently being prospectively evaluated in PelvEx II, a randomized phase 3 trial comparing induction chemotherapy followed by chemotherapy&#x02010;reRT and surgery versus chemotherapy&#x02010;reRT and surgery alone in patients with locally recurrent rectal cancer.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0041" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>41</sup>
</xref>
</p></sec><sec id="caac70016-sec-0040"><title>PATIENT SELECTION FOR REIRRADIATION</title><p>Several key clinical criteria may serve to consistently guide patient selection across various tumor sites (Table&#x000a0;<xref rid="caac70016-tbl-0002" ref-type="table">2</xref>) and help clinicians and patients engage in an informed joint decision&#x02010;making process to achieve the best possible outcome.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0001" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>1</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="caac70016-bib-0004" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>4</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="caac70016-bib-0032" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>32</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="caac70016-bib-0042" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>42</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="caac70016-bib-0043" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>43</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="caac70016-bib-0044" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>44</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="caac70016-bib-0045" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>45</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="caac70016-bib-0046" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>46</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="caac70016-bib-0047" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>47</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="caac70016-bib-0048" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>48</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="caac70016-bib-0049" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>49</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="caac70016-bib-0050" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>50</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="caac70016-bib-0051" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>51</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="caac70016-bib-0052" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>52</sup>
</xref>
</p><table-wrap position="float" id="caac70016-tbl-0002" content-type="TABLE"><label>TABLE 2</label><caption><p>Main criteria for patient selection for reirradiation.</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><col align="left" span="1"/><col align="left" span="1"/><col align="left" span="1"/><col align="left" span="1"/><col align="left" span="1"/><thead valign="bottom"><tr style="border-bottom:solid 1px #000000"><th align="left" valign="bottom" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Organ site (references)</th><th align="left" valign="bottom" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Patient fitness</th><th align="left" valign="bottom" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Interval between irradiations</th><th align="left" valign="bottom" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Comorbidities and adequate organ function</th><th align="left" valign="bottom" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Risk of side effects</th></tr></thead><tbody valign="top"><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Lung cancer (Andratschke 2022,<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0001" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>1</sup>
</xref> Rulach 2021<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0042" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>42</sup>
</xref>)</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">ECOG PS 0&#x02013;2 (ECOG PS 3 for SBRT if nonrespiratory decline)</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02265;6 months</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Avoid in patients with interstitial lung disease</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>
<italic toggle="yes">Life&#x02010;threatening:</italic> Central mediastinal structures (e.g., esophagus, bronchial tree, large vessels) with risks of stenosis, fistula, or bleeding</p>
<p>
<italic toggle="yes">Functional:</italic> Respiratory impairment from large reRT volumes in peripheral tumors</p>
</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Head and neck cancer (Andratschke 2022,<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0001" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>1</sup>
</xref> Ng 2021<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0043" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>43</sup>
</xref>)</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">ECOG PS 0&#x02013;2</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02265;6 months (minimum), &#x0003e;12 months (recommended)</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Avoid in patients with severe dysphagia (feeding tube dependency) and tracheal dyspnea (tracheostomy)</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>
<italic toggle="yes">Life&#x02010;threatening:</italic> Carotid artery (vascular rupture), brainstem (vital functions)</p>
<p>
<italic toggle="yes">Functional:</italic> Neurologic structures like the optic chiasm (vision loss); deglutition structures, leading to swallowing difficulties and reduced quality of life</p>
</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">High&#x02010;grade glioma (Andratschke 2025,<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0044" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>44</sup>
</xref> Ryu 2014<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0045" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>45</sup>
</xref>)</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">ECOG PS 0&#x02013;1</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x0003e;6 months</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Avoid if cognitive impairment or significant visual deficits are present</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>
<italic toggle="yes">Life&#x02010;threatening:</italic> Brainstem (vital functions)</p>
<p>
<italic toggle="yes">Functional:</italic> Optic chiasm (vision loss); large reirradiated brain volumes increase the risk of cognitive impairment, significantly affecting quality of life</p>
</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Breast cancer (Andratschke 2022,<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0001" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>1</sup>
</xref> Hannoun&#x02010;Levi 2023<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0032" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>32</sup>
</xref>)</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">ECOG PS 0&#x02013;2</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x0003e;12 months (36 months for second breast&#x02010;conservative treatment)</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Avoid if cumulative dose tolerance is exceeded or if significant prior RT side effects&#x02014;particularly aesthetic effects&#x02014;are present</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>
<italic toggle="yes">Functional:</italic> Structures near the brachial plexus (sensory and motor deficits)</p>
<p>
<italic toggle="yes">Aesthetic and psychosocial:</italic> Induration and aesthetic changes, notably in in&#x02010;breast recurrences</p>
</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Prostate cancer (Andratschke 2022,<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0001" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>1</sup>
</xref> Jereczek&#x02010;Fossa 2021,<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0046" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>46</sup>
</xref> Kaljouw 2016,<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0047" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>47</sup>
</xref> Slevin 2021<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0048" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>48</sup>
</xref>)</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">ECOG PS 0&#x02013;1</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x0003e;24 months</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Limit IPSS to 15</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<italic toggle="yes">Functional:</italic> Bladder and rectum (e.g., incontinence, bleeding), affecting quality of life</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Rectal cancer (Guren 2014,<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0049" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>49</sup>
</xref> Tao 2018,<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0024" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>24</sup>
</xref> Mantello 2023<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0050" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>50</sup>
</xref>)</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">ECOG PS 0&#x02013;2</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02265;6 months (minimum), &#x0003e;12 months (commonly considered)</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Avoid in patients with poor baseline gastrointestinal function or extensive prior pelvic surgery</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>
<italic toggle="yes">Life&#x02010;threatening:</italic> Fistula formation and presacral abscesses, particularly after surgery</p>
<p>
<italic toggle="yes">Functional:</italic> Chronic pain, bowel or bladder dysfunction, and neuropathy</p>
</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Cervical cancer (Weitmann 2006,<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0051" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>51</sup>
</xref> Mabuchi 2014,<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0052" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>52</sup>
</xref> Sturdza 2016<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0004" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>4</sup>
</xref>)</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">ECOG PS 0&#x02013;1</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02265;6 months (minimum), &#x0003e;12 months (commonly considered)</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Avoid in patients with poor baseline gastrointestinal or genitourinary function, extensive prior pelvic surgery, or severe radiation&#x02010;induced fibrosis</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>
<italic toggle="yes">Life&#x02010;threatening:</italic> Severe fistula formation (rectovaginal, vesicovaginal), bladder or rectal necrosis</p>
<p>
<italic toggle="yes">Functional:</italic> Chronic vaginal ulcers, cystitis, severe pain, and bowel dysfunction significantly affecting quality of life</p>
</td></tr></tbody></table><table-wrap-foot id="caac70016-ntgp-0002"><fn id="caac70016-note-0003"><p>Abbreviations: ECOG PS, Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group performance status; IPSS, International Prostate Symptom Score; reRT, reirradiation; RT, radiation therapy; SBRT, stereotactic body radiation therapy.</p></fn></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap><sec id="caac70016-sec-0050"><title>Assessment of patients' eligibility for reRT</title><p>PS plays a major role in predicting positive outcomes, and patients who have a high PS (Karnofsky PS &#x02265;80 or Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group [ECOG] PS &#x02264;2, indicating normal daily functioning) are more suitable candidates for reRT (Table&#x000a0;<xref rid="caac70016-tbl-0002" ref-type="table">2</xref>).<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0001" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>1</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="caac70016-bib-0004" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>4</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="caac70016-bib-0032" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>32</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="caac70016-bib-0042" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>42</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="caac70016-bib-0043" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>43</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="caac70016-bib-0044" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>44</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="caac70016-bib-0045" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>45</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="caac70016-bib-0046" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>46</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="caac70016-bib-0047" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>47</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="caac70016-bib-0048" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>48</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="caac70016-bib-0049" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>49</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="caac70016-bib-0050" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>50</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="caac70016-bib-0051" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>51</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="caac70016-bib-0052" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>52</sup>
</xref> This has been demonstrated in randomized clinical trials on nasopharyngeal cancer<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0020" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>20</sup>
</xref> and brain tumors,<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0029" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>29</sup>
</xref> in which a high PS was consistently associated with improved OS. A similar association was observed in a retrospective study of 103 patients treated with reRT and concurrent chemotherapy for locally recurrent rectal cancer in which patients with a Karnofsky PS &#x0003e;70% experienced significantly improved OS (<italic toggle="yes">p</italic>&#x000a0;&#x0003c;&#x000a0;.0005).<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0053" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>53</sup>
</xref> In palliative settings, reRT still may be appropriate for patients with a low PS when the primary goal is symptom relief. Notably, in a randomized trial that compared two fractionation schedules for reRT in bone metastases, approximately 25% of patients had an ECOG PS of 2, and no increase in side effects was observed in this subgroup.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0025" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>25</sup>
</xref>
</p><p>Comorbidities and adequate organ function are also important factors. For instance, patients with significantly reduced lung function are often excluded from reRT because of the heightened risks of pneumonitis and fibrosis<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0054" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>54</sup>
</xref>; and in HNC, functional impairments such as severe dysphagia and tracheal dyspnea might disqualify patients.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0035" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>35</sup>
</xref> Comorbidities such as diabetes may also influence the risk profile associated with reRT.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0055" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>55</sup>
</xref>
</p></sec><sec id="caac70016-sec-0060"><title>Life expectancy and predicted treatment efficacy</title><p>It is necessary to assess whether a patient is likely to benefit from reRT, considering both life expectancy and the expected effectiveness of the treatment. Most studies have excluded patients who have distant metastases at diagnosis when reRT was delivered with curative intent, making it difficult to determine its effect on OS in this population. For example, in most retrospective studies evaluating reRT for breast or lung cancer, patients with metastases at diagnosis were excluded from analysis.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0056" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>56</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="caac70016-bib-0057" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>57</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="caac70016-bib-0058" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>58</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="caac70016-bib-0059" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>59</sup>
</xref> Notably, one randomized trial in breast cancer did include 24 patients with distant metastases at diagnosis for analysis and did not identify this as a prognostic factor for survival, highlighting the lack of robust evidence supporting its predictive role.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0031" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>31</sup>
</xref>
</p><p>The interval between the initial radiation course and reRT has been consistently shown to be an important prognostic factor. For breast cancer, a multicenter retrospective study using propensity score matching demonstrated that patients with an interval greater than 36 months between irradiations had significantly improved OS (HR, 1.78; 95% confidence interval [CI], 1.02&#x02013;3.08; <italic toggle="yes">p</italic>&#x000a0;=&#x000a0;0.035).<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0059" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>59</sup>
</xref> A similar finding was reported in patients with HNC, in whom an interval exceeding 2 years was associated with better survival outcomes (HR, 1.5; <italic toggle="yes">p</italic>&#x000a0;=&#x000a0;0.028).<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0035" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>35</sup>
</xref> In cervical cancer, a retrospective study of 52 patients treated with high&#x02010;dose&#x02010;rate interstitial brachytherapy for central pelvic recurrence demonstrated that a treatment&#x02010;free interval &#x0003c;6 months was significantly associated with worse OS (HR, 0.25; 95% CI, 0.08&#x02013;0.76; <italic toggle="yes">p</italic>&#x000a0;=&#x000a0;0.015).<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0052" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>52</sup>
</xref>
</p><p>In addition to the treatment interval, tumor size has been identified as a significant prognostic factor for survival across multiple tumor types. In breast cancer, for example, a tumor size &#x02265;30 mm was associated with a significantly increased risk of mortality (HR, 3.60; 95% CI, 1.19&#x02013;10.88; <italic toggle="yes">p</italic>&#x000a0;=&#x000a0;0.023) in a multicenter retrospective study.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0059" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>59</sup>
</xref> Similarly, in HNC, a multicenter retrospective study demonstrated that a tumor volume &#x0003e;26.0 cm<sup>3</sup> was associated with worse OS versus smaller tumors (HR, 1.7; 95% CI, 1.01&#x02013;2.91; <italic toggle="yes">p</italic>&#x000a0;=&#x000a0;0.05).<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0017" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>17</sup>
</xref> In the same cervical cancer cohort, a maximum tumor dimension &#x02265;40 mm was also significantly associated with poorer OS (HR, 3.26; 95% CI, 1.24&#x02013;8.54; <italic toggle="yes">p</italic>&#x000a0;=&#x000a0;0.017).<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0052" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>52</sup>
</xref>
</p></sec><sec id="caac70016-sec-0070"><title>Evaluation of risk factors for reRT&#x02010;related side effects</title><p>The risk of severe side effects also must be carefully weighed (Table&#x000a0;<xref rid="caac70016-tbl-0002" ref-type="table">2</xref>). Although many key organs are thought to have limited recovery after radiation (see Radiobiologic consideration, below), evidence suggests that the spinal cord may exhibit some degree of time&#x02010;dependent recovery. A systematic review analyzing 40 patients across eight reports highlighted this potential for recovery, emphasizing that extending the interval between radiation courses beyond 6 months appeared to significantly reduce the risk of radiation&#x02010;induced myelopathy.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0060" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>60</sup>
</xref> In addition, evaluating the effects of previous RT, both subjective and objective, is essential for identifying appropriate candidates for reRT. This includes a thorough assessment of previous side effects, such as chronic pain, fibrosis, or functional impairments, which may increase the risk of further side effects. Furthermore, the overlap of new treatment volumes with previously irradiated volumes and the proximity of critical structures&#x02014;such as the spinal cord, esophagus, heart, and major airways&#x02014;are crucial factors influencing the risk of severe side effects (Table&#x000a0;<xref rid="caac70016-tbl-0002" ref-type="table">2</xref>). Additional factors, such as prior surgery, chemotherapy, or infections, can significantly increase the severity of late reRT side effects. For example, prior surgery and infections have been associated with higher risks of carotid blowout or fistula formation in head and neck and pelvic reRT.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0061" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>61</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="caac70016-bib-0062" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>62</sup>
</xref> Also, patients with extensive previous receipt of chemotherapy, particularly in the pelvic setting, may exhibit heightened radiosensitivity because of impaired tissue repair.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0063" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>63</sup>
</xref> These factors must be carefully evaluated in the context of reRT because they can heighten the susceptibility of normal tissues to additional radiation damage. Individual radiosensitivity has also been increasingly recognized, with ongoing efforts to predict patient&#x02010;specific responses using biomarkers.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0064" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>64</sup>
</xref>
</p></sec><sec id="caac70016-sec-0080"><title>Radiobiologic considerations</title><p>Radiobiologic considerations are critical in the planning of reRT because of the complexity of tissue response after initial RT. A key consideration in the clinical management of reRT is avoiding cumulative doses that may lead to severe side effects. According to best practices outlined in the ESTRO/EORTC consensus,<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0001" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>1</sup>
</xref> cumulative dose is indeed considered a key determinant of the safe delivery of reRT, with the risk of side effects depending not only on the total radiation dose received but also on factors such as tissue recovery potential, fractionation schedules, irradiated volume, residual radiation side effects from earlier treatment, and the structural characteristics of the affected organs.</p><p>The historical fractionation schedules, for which radiosensitivity is well characterized, use from 1.8 to 2.0 Gy per fraction once daily,<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0065" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>65</sup>
</xref> with most RT courses delivered over 3&#x02013;7 weeks. These schedules are commonly applied both for primary (de novo) RT and reRT. Variations from this standard include hypofractionation, which uses larger doses per fraction over fewer sessions, and hyperfractionation, which involves lower doses per fraction administered more frequently. Given the variation in fractionation regimens, the concept of the <italic toggle="yes">equivalent effective dose in 2&#x02010;Gy fractions</italic> (EQD2Gy) is essential for evaluating cumulative dose exposure. This measure provides an estimate of the corresponding total dose delivered in 2&#x02010;Gy fractions to achieve the same biologic effect as the dose/fractionation schedule under consideration. It allows for standardization of biologically effective doses across different treatment courses, facilitating meaningful comparisons between prior RT and reRT plans. By summing the EQD2Gy across treatments, clinicians can estimate the cumulative biologic effect on normal tissues and assess the risk of late side effects.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0066" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>66</sup>
</xref> Importantly, EQD2Gy estimates are tissue&#x02010;specific and end point&#x02013;specific, and multiple EQD2Gy calculations may be needed for an individual patient and treatment to provide a comprehensive assessment of the risk of side effects from cumulative dose exposure.</p><p>From a radiobiologic perspective, the response of normal tissues to reRT varies, depending on the underlying tissue architecture and function.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0067" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>67</sup>
</xref> Some organs have critical dose&#x02010;limiting side effects. which are driven primarily by localized cumulative high&#x02010;dose exposure, in which damage to even a small portion of these structures can lead to significant functional impairment. Examples include the spinal cord, brainstem, and bowel. By contrast, other organs, such as the lungs, liver, and kidneys, exhibit a greater degree of functional reserve, with the risk of side effects depending more on the total irradiated volume rather than a single high&#x02010;dose focal region. These considerations are fundamental in guiding dose constraints during reRT planning. Moreover, these categories are not mutually exclusive. Normal tissue response is end point&#x02013;specific; that is, a single organ may exhibit both <italic toggle="yes">serial</italic> and <italic toggle="yes">parallel</italic> behavior, depending on the type of side effect under consideration. This complexity is particularly relevant when evaluating cumulative dose distributions in the reRT setting.</p><p>Dose constraints for normal tissues in the de novo setting have been established through a combination of clinical observations, radiobiologic modeling, and retrospective analyses. Large&#x02010;scale initiatives, such as Quantitative Analyses of Normal Tissue Effects in the Clinic, have played a critical role in defining acceptable dose limits, with normal tissue complication probability models providing additional insights into the likelihood of severe toxicity based on dose&#x02013;volume relationships.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0068" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>68</sup>
</xref> For many of the key dose&#x02010;limiting organs in the reRT setting, dose constraints primarily are based on limiting maximum point doses, such as the highest dose received by any point within the organ (Dmax) or the highest tolerable dose delivered to the most exposed 0.1 cubic centimeter (D0.1cc). Where organs and side effect outcomes are primarily driven by volume effects and functional reserve, constraints are more commonly defined in terms of volume&#x02010;based parameters, such as the mean dose and the volume of an organ that should be protected and exposed to a lower dose to minimize widespread organ dysfunction.</p><p>Although dose constraints for a first course of RT are well established and supported by robust clinical data, cumulative dose thresholds for reRT remain poorly defined and are not based on high&#x02010;level evidence. In the absence of prospective, data&#x02010;driven guidelines, current recommendations rely on expert consensus. Table&#x000a0;<xref rid="caac70016-tbl-0003" ref-type="table">3</xref>
<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0042" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>42</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="caac70016-bib-0043" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>43</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="caac70016-bib-0048" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>48</sup>
</xref> summarizes the cumulative dose guidance proposed by international expert groups, serving as a conservative, practical reference to guide clinical decision making in reRT settings. Moreover, a recent study analyzing nearly 3000 external&#x02010;beam reRT courses delivered at the University of Michigan since 2017 provides valuable insight into how dose constraints are implemented in practice within a high&#x02010;volume center.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0069" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>69</sup>
</xref> These institutional data, although not representing validated international guidelines, highlight how clinicians adapt to the complexity of reRT planning. In 17% of cases examined in the most recent year of the Michigan cohort, dose constraints were exceeded because of various clinical considerations, with the most frequent reasons being the prioritization of tumor coverage over normal tissue dose limits and uncertainties in cumulative dose estimation because of image registration challenges (see Radiation Physics Considerations for Reirradiation, below). In some instances, the minimal overlap between the initial and repeat RT fields allowed physicians to rely on primary treatment constraints rather than cumulative limits. Conversely, in other cases, dose thresholds had already been surpassed in previous treatments, requiring individualized risk&#x02010;benefit assessments to guide retreatment decisions.</p><table-wrap position="float" id="caac70016-tbl-0003" content-type="TABLE"><label>TABLE 3</label><caption><p>Expert consensus&#x02013;based cumulative dose guidance for normal tissue in reirradiation.</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><col align="left" span="1"/><col align="left" span="1"/><col align="left" span="1"/><col align="left" span="1"/><col align="left" span="1"/><thead valign="bottom"><tr style="border-bottom:solid 1px #000000"><th align="left" valign="bottom" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Organ</th><th align="left" valign="bottom" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Desirable cumulative EQD2Gy<xref rid="caac70016-note-0005" ref-type="table-fn">
<sup>a</sup>
</xref>
