<sup>42</sup>
</xref>
</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Bladder</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">D<sub>0.5cc</sub>&#x000a0;&#x02264;&#x000a0;80 Gy</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">D<sub>0.5cc</sub>&#x000a0;&#x0003c;&#x000a0;110 Gy</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">3</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Slevin 2021<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0048" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>48</sup>
</xref>
</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Small bowel</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">D<sub>0.5cc</sub>&#x000a0;&#x02264;&#x000a0;70 Gy</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">D<sub>0.5cc</sub>&#x000a0;&#x0003c;&#x000a0;90 Gy</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">3</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Slevin 2021<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0048" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>48</sup>
</xref>
</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Colon/sigmoid/rectum</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">D<sub>0.5cc</sub>&#x000a0;&#x02264;&#x000a0;80 Gy</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">D<sub>0.5cc</sub>&#x000a0;&#x0003c;&#x000a0;100 Gy</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">3</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Slevin 2021<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0048" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>48</sup>
</xref>
</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Cauda equina/sacral plexus</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">D<sub>1.0cc</sub>&#x000a0;&#x02264;&#x000a0;67 Gy</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">D<sub>1.0cc</sub>&#x000a0;&#x0003c;&#x000a0;85 Gy</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">2</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Slevin 2021<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0048" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>48</sup>
</xref>
</td></tr></tbody></table><table-wrap-foot id="caac70016-ntgp-0003"><fn id="caac70016-note-0004"><p>Abbreviations: D<sub>0.03cc</sub>/D<sub>0.5cc</sub>/D<sub>1cc</sub>, dose to the most exposed 0.03/0.5/1.0 cm<sup>3</sup>, respectively; Dmax, maximum dose; EQD2Gy, equivalent dose in 2&#x02010;gray fractions; Gy, grays.</p></fn><fn id="caac70016-note-0005"><label>
<sup>a</sup>
</label><p>All values are expressed as the EQD2Gy, calculated using the linear&#x02010;quadratic model. The &#x003b1;/&#x003b2; value used was either specified in the original source or was assumed to be 2&#x02013;3 Gy for late&#x02010;responding normal tissues. Desirable and acceptable cumulative dose limits are reported when available based on international expert consensus guidelines. Although tissue recovery between radiation courses is a key consideration in reirradiation planning, assumptions regarding recovery vary widely across studies and were not incorporated systematically into this table. Instead, readers are referred to the original publications for details on how recovery may have been accounted for when deriving the listed thresholds. These values are not intended as prescriptive constraints but, rather, as cautious, consensus&#x02010;based guidance to support clinical judgment in reirradiation decision making.</p></fn></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap><p>As detailed above, the interval between initial RT and reRT plays a major role in balancing the safety and efficacy of the latter because longer intervals may allow for greater tissue recovery and reduce the risk of cumulative side effects. In nonirradiated tissues, the severity of damage after RT can range from subclinical alterations to significant loss of parenchymal function. The ability of tissues to regenerate depends largely on the presence of stem cells capable of repopulating affected areas; rapidly proliferating tissues&#x02014;such as mucous membranes, skin, and the rectum&#x02014;demonstrate a higher likelihood of regeneration.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0070" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>70</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="caac70016-bib-0071" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>71</sup>
</xref> In contrast, regeneration in slowly proliferating tissues is more complex. Beyond proliferation, the ability to repair sublethal and potentially lethal damage is also critical to regeneration.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0070" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>70</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="caac70016-bib-0071" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>71</sup>
</xref> The lower the dose and irradiated volume of the normal tissue, the higher the rate of the delivered dose, which can be compensated by the repair processes.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0072" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>72</sup>
</xref> Consequently, a <italic toggle="yes">recovery</italic> factor appears to exist for some tissues in which the risk of side effects from reRT may decrease over time, potentially nearing the levels observed in previously untreated patients. This effect, thought to result from tissue recovery after a significant interval since the initial dose, has been demonstrated primarily in the central nervous system,<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0073" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>73</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="caac70016-bib-0074" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>74</sup>
</xref> in which extended intervals between RT courses are associated with a lower incidence of severe side effects.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0074" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>74</sup>
</xref> It should be recognized that, whereas some tissues demonstrate meaningful recovery potential, this is often insufficient to prevent late radiation effects in organs with inherently low regenerative capacity; and, in certain tissues, cumulative dose tolerance may remain low regardless of the interval between treatments.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0075" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>75</sup>
</xref>
</p><p>It is important to consider the possibility of acquired radioresistance in tumors undergoing reRT. Although direct clinical evidence remains limited, it is plausible that this phenomenon occurs through cellular mechanisms, such as mutations that enhance DNA repair capacity, or at the tumor level, where initial RT may eradicate the more radiosensitive cells, leaving a more resistant subpopulation to drive recurrence.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0076" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>76</sup>
</xref> Altered fractionation schedules, such as hypofractionation, are hypothesized to help overcome such resistance by allowing higher doses to be delivered safely within the constraints of normal tissue tolerance.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0077" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>77</sup>
</xref>
</p><p>Overall, the biologic effects of reRT depend on multiple factors, including the time interval between RT courses, the dose&#x02013;volume relationships, and the ability of tissues to recover. Although the concept of tissue recovery offers hope for delivering additional radiation safely, the risks must be carefully balanced against the potential for tumor control.</p></sec><sec id="caac70016-sec-0090"><title>Considerations for patient&#x02010;centered decision making in reRT</title><p>In reRT, substantial uncertainties regarding both treatment outcomes and associated risks affect decision making and the informed&#x02010;consent process. In particular, the difficulties in precisely predicting life&#x02010;threatening side effects and the long&#x02010;term consequences of side&#x02010;effects (such as chronic pain, aesthetic changes, and functional impairments) should be communicated transparently, ensuring that patients are aware of both the possible benefits and the risks of reRT. This approach fosters informed decision making, enabling a more holistic understanding of patient needs and supporting ethically grounded, patient&#x02010;centered care.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0078" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>78</sup>
</xref>
</p></sec></sec><sec id="caac70016-sec-0100"><title>ADVANCED IMAGING FOR TARGET VOLUME DEFINITION</title><sec id="caac70016-sec-0110"><title>Target volume delineation in reRT</title><p>Effective delivery of RT depends completely on accurately identifying the anatomic areas to be treated, termed the <italic toggle="yes">target volumes</italic>, which are outlined on pretreatment imaging to guide RT planning and delivery. However, although there is well established international consensus on target volume definitions for most cancers in the primary RT context, one survey highlighted significant heterogeneity in defining target volumes for reRT.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0010" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>10</sup>
</xref> Despite this, one consistent approach across tumor sites is the principle of minimizing the target volume to reduce radiation exposure to adjacent, healthy tissues. Unlike initial RT, which may include treating surrounding areas to address potential microscopic disease, reRT typically targets the visible tumor with carefully minimized margins. The clinical target volume (CTV), either if used at all or, typically, used to cover potential microscopic spread, is often much smaller in reRT than in initial RT. This represents a careful balancing between the potential benefits of additional treatment of microscopic tumor spread (and hence an increased probability of disease control) and the increased risk of side effects with larger treatment volumes. In practice, a slightly larger volume than the tumor alone is also irradiated to account for uncertainties and daily variations in patient and tumor positioning, including physiologic functions like respiratory motion. This planning target volume can be minimized through daily image&#x02010;guided RT, in which imaging at each session allows precise adaptation to the patient's position, focusing RT on the tumor and sparing healthy tissues. In reRT, reducing the size of the planning target volume as much as possible remains essential to minimize the risk of complications to surrounding organs.</p></sec><sec id="caac70016-sec-0120"><title>Challenges in imaging for reRT</title><p>The definition of gross tumor volume (GTV), representing the visible tumor mass, poses significant challenges in the context of reRT, particularly when differentiating between recurrence and treatment&#x02010;induced changes from the initial RT. One study has highlighted several typical commonly observed posttreatment imaging changes.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0079" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>79</sup>
</xref> For example, in the central nervous system, pseudoprogression frequently occurs in high&#x02010;grade gliomas within months of completing RT, making it particularly challenging to distinguish between tumor progression and these transient posttreatment changes, coined <italic toggle="yes">pseudoprogression</italic>. Advanced magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) techniques, such as apparent diffusion coefficient sequences are often useful to differentiate between these conditions because pseudoprogression generally shows higher apparent diffusion coefficient values and a reduction in total tumor volume in contrast to tumor progression. In addition, late&#x02010;onset RT&#x02010;induced vascular lesions, such as progressive cerebral arteriopathy and parenchymal damage&#x02014;including leukoencephalopathy and radionecrosis&#x02014;can mimic tumor recurrence on imaging, posing challenges for accurate target volume delineation. In these cases, integrating such advanced imaging techniques as perfusion MRI or fluorine&#x02010;18 (<sup>18</sup>F)&#x02013;labeled dihydroxyphenylalanine (<sup>18</sup>F&#x02010;DOPA) positron emission tomography (PET) may help to establish a reliable differential diagnosis.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0080" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>80</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="caac70016-bib-0081" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>81</sup>
</xref> In HNC, radiation&#x02010;induced side effects are varied, including thickening of superficial soft tissues, deep anatomic space edema, and bone necrosis, particularly osteoradionecrosis.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0082" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>82</sup>
</xref> These alterations can persist for years after initial treatment and often are mistaken for signs of tumor recurrence.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0083" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>83</sup>
</xref> In the lungs, radiation&#x02010;induced damage presents as acute pneumonitis or late fibrosis, whereas systemic treatments can lead to capillary leak syndromes, interstitial pneumonia, or pulmonary hemorrhages.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0084" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>84</sup>
</xref> In rectal cancer, the delineation of recurrence is complicated by postoperative anatomic distortion, fibrosis, and chronic inflammation. Multiparametric MRI, and particularly diffusion&#x02010;weighted imaging, plays a critical role in distinguishing viable tumor tissue from treatment&#x02010;related changes, especially in small or anatomically ambiguous recurrences (Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="caac70016-fig-0003" ref-type="fig">3</xref>).<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0085" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>85</sup>
</xref> In gynecologic cancers, posttreatment changes, such as uterine atrophy, cervical fibrosis, and ovarian shrinkage, can similarly mimic recurrence. Here too, multiparametric pelvic MRI, often complemented by diffusion&#x02010;weighted imaging, is the preferred modality to differentiate postradiation effects from residual or recurrent disease.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0086" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>86</sup>
</xref> This issue underscores the potential role of emerging imaging techniques, which may provide useful information to distinguish recurrence from posttreatment effects. An example of this challenge is seen in Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="caac70016-fig-0004" ref-type="fig">4</xref>, in which <sup>18</sup>F&#x02010;DOPA PET imaging helped distinguish between radionecrosis and tumor progression in the brain.</p><fig position="float" fig-type="FIGURE" id="caac70016-fig-0003"><label>FIGURE 3</label><caption><p>Multiparametric pelvic MRI illustrates a local recurrence 8 months after the completion of chemoradiotherapy and a watch&#x02010;and&#x02010;wait strategy in a patient with rectal cancer. (A)&#x000a0;An axial T2&#x02010;weighted MRI shows subtle tissue irregularity in the anterolateral anal canal and lower rectovaginal septum, but the lesion remains poorly conspicuous. (B)&#x000a0;A diffusion&#x02010;weighted image highlights a focal area of markedly restricted diffusion (green arrow), confirming the presence of a 3&#x02010;cm recurrence. Diffusion&#x02010;weighted imaging was instrumental in detecting the lesion in this anatomically complex, previously irradiated area. MRI indicates magnetic resonance imaging.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="CAAC-75-630-g008" position="anchor" id="jats-graphic-5"/></fig><fig position="float" fig-type="FIGURE" id="caac70016-fig-0004"><label>FIGURE 4</label><caption><p>Tumor recurrence versus brain radionecrosis in a patient aged 75 years who was previously irradiated for a right ethmoid adenocarcinoma. (A)&#x000a0;Gadolinium&#x02010;enhanced T1&#x02010;weighted MRI revealed a right frontal tissue mass, (B,C) which was confirmed as tumor recurrence by hypercaptation on <sup>18</sup>F&#x02010;DOPA PET imaging. Subtotal resection confirmed the recurrence, and the patient underwent reRT to the residual tumor. <sup>18</sup>F&#x02010;DOPA indicates fluorine&#x02010;18&#x02013;labeled dihydroxyphenylalanine; MRI, magnetic resonance imaging; PET, positron emission tomography; reRT, reirradiation.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="CAAC-75-630-g003" position="anchor" id="jats-graphic-7"/></fig></sec></sec><sec id="caac70016-sec-0130"><title>RADIATION PHYSICS CONSIDERATIONS FOR REIRRADIATION</title><p>ReRT treatment planning should account for the previously delivered RT, with the level of complexity depending on the previous treatment information available. Where feasible, sophisticated treatment&#x02010;planning techniques and software, as well as a structured approach for cumulative dose evaluation, should be used.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0087" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>87</sup>
</xref> Key challenges include dose summation, dose mapping through image registration, and equieffective biologic dose estimation for reliable cumulative dose optimization and assessment.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0088" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>88</sup>
</xref>
</p><p>Safe delivery of reRT depends on a robust evaluation of cumulative doses to surrounding normal tissues previously exposed to clinically relevant radiation doses. This represents one of the most complex technical problems in reRT, especially in the presence of intertreatment and intratreatment anatomic changes. Patients may experience significant anatomic changes between treatment courses, often months or years apart, including weight loss/gain, surgery, and radiation&#x02010;induced tissue changes (Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="caac70016-fig-0005" ref-type="fig">5</xref>). Various methods of cumulative dose evaluation are used in clinical practice, including visual evaluation of dose distribution overlays, manual read&#x02010;out and summation of maximum dose to specific normal tissue, and full three&#x02010;dimensional (3D) co&#x02010;registration of image sets to transfer dose distributions between imaging done at different time points.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0089" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>89</sup>
</xref>
</p><fig position="float" fig-type="FIGURE" id="caac70016-fig-0005"><label>FIGURE 5</label><caption><p>Morphologic challenges in head and neck cancer reRT. Challenges for image registration are highlighted between the initial RT and the reRT planning CT scans because of significant changes in patient morphology. The top row (sagittal views) shows a first patient with (A)&#x000a0;an initial laryngeal tumor, (B)&#x000a0;a new oral cavity tumor 9 years after RT, with a markedly stooped posture, and (C) the corresponding registration between both scans, illustrating the difficulties encountered. The bottom row (axial views) shows a second patient with (D) an initial parotid tumor, (E)&#x000a0;a recurrence in the same location 6 years later, with postsurgical changes, including a reconstructed mandible with a metal insert (orange), and (F) the resulting registration used for reRT planning, which was particularly challenging. CT indicates computed tomography; reRT, reirradiation; RT, radiation therapy.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="CAAC-75-630-g001" position="anchor" id="jats-graphic-9"/></fig><p>Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="caac70016-fig-0006" ref-type="fig">6</xref> summarizes the steps required for 3D dose distribution summation. First, a global alignment of imaging from the previous treatment to the current imaging is achieved using rigid image registration (RIR). RIR only uses translation and rotation in the 3D space. In patients who have experienced complex anatomic changes (Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="caac70016-fig-0005" ref-type="fig">5</xref>), RIR may not be sufficient to fully align imaging from previous and current treatments. Consequently, deformable image registration (DIR) is used increasingly as a second step to enable local alignment of corresponding anatomy.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0090" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>90</sup>
</xref> DIR is an advanced technique that can accommodate complex deformations and movements within the body, such as organ motion, patient positioning differences, and tissue deformation. In one international survey, RIR was used 68%&#x02013;77% of the time, depending on body site, whereas DIR was used 33%&#x02013;46% of the time.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0010" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>10</sup>
</xref> After image registration, dose distributions from previous treatment courses are mapped onto the current anatomy and summed, taking radiobiologic considerations into account. This may include a rescaling of dose to account for any tissue recovery in addition to correction for fraction&#x02010;size effects resulting from the specific treatment schedules used. Typically, a conversion to the EQD2Gy is used,<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0091" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>91</sup>
</xref> as described above. When 3D image registration and dose mapping are used, this rescaling must be applied to the whole 3D dose matrix. Because radiobiologic effects are local and vary across space, they depend on the type of normal tissue considered as well as the specific dose delivered to a given point. This further complicates the evaluation of the cumulative dose and represents a significant barrier to routine clinical use of reRT.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0092" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>92</sup>
</xref>
</p><fig position="float" fig-type="FIGURE" id="caac70016-fig-0006"><label>FIGURE 6</label><caption><p>Steps for dose summation in reRT treatment planning. (A)&#x000a0;Image registration, starting with rigid alignment of the initial (CT1) and reRT (CT2) data sets, followed by deformable registration, with vectors linking corresponding locations. (B)&#x000a0;Dose mapping, projecting the initial dose distribution onto the reRT CT (CT2). (C)&#x000a0;Plan summation in EQD2Gy, accounting for fraction&#x02010;size effects and any DSE, such as tissue recovery, to estimate the cumulative dose. CT indicates computed tomography; DSE, dose&#x02010;scaling factor; EQD2Gy equivalent dose in 2&#x02010;gray fractions; reRT, reirradiation.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="CAAC-75-630-g004" position="anchor" id="jats-graphic-11"/></fig><p>The estimated cumulative dose can be highly dependent on the method used for dose accumulation. A study assessed the consistency of cumulative healthy tissue dose estimation across different clinical pathways for reRT cases,<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0089" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>89</sup>
</xref> and the results showed significant interobserver variation in near&#x02010;maximum cumulative doses, underscoring the need for standardized workflows, with DIR methods potentially offering more consistent outcomes. The summation of near&#x02010;maximum point doses is more straightforward than the use of image registration methods and does not require complete information about the prior delivered dose. However, it may result in overly conservative estimates of the total dose and thus potentially lower (and less radical) doses prescribed to the reRT treatment target. Conversely, whereas robust 3D dose summation in EQD2Gy offers the potential for a more accurate cumulative dose estimation, this technology is not yet available in all RT treatment&#x02010;planning software systems.</p><p>Evaluating cumulative doses requires an accurate identification of individual normal tissues on imaging through the contouring of healthy organs and organ substructures and often extending beyond those considered in the de&#x02010;novo setting. For example, some studies suggest a link between a dose to the carotid arteries, which are not normally considered in the initial RT course,<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0093" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>93</sup>
</xref> and a higher risk of developing carotid blowout syndrome after head and neck reRT.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0094" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>94</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="caac70016-bib-0095" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>95</sup>
</xref> Inconsistencies in organ definition, such as with the heart&#x02014;which may include/exclude the pericardial sac or part of the artery arch&#x02014;introduce uncertainties in the assessment of cumulative doses. Automatic segmentation of healthy organs using deep&#x02010;learning methods has permeated RT clinical practice and will certainly foster a harmonized delineation of organs at risk.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0096" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>96</sup>
</xref> However, the performance of these artificial intelligence&#x02010;based software solutions may be hampered by postradiogenic or other treatment&#x02010;related changes and may appear or be <italic toggle="yes">atypical</italic> for models trained on normal anatomy. Further evaluation of such tools in the reRT setting to improve the consistency of cumulative dose assessment is required.</p><p>ReRT treatment planning also needs specialist technical considerations. Ideally, an reRT treatment plan should take into account the previously delivered normal tissue dose distribution, sparing areas previously treated to a high dose. This process is not automated and, in practice, often involves several manual iterative steps, with multiple intermediate rounds of cumulative dose evaluation, and draws considerably on RT department resources. This represents another significant barrier to routine reRT delivery. Work in this space has looked at the options for automated planning through scripting<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0097" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>97</sup>
</xref> or using dedicated optimization cost functions for plan optimization,<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0098" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>98</sup>
</xref> but this is not yet integrated into commercially available treatment&#x02010;planning software packages.</p></sec><sec id="caac70016-sec-0140"><title>TECHNIQUES IN REIRRADIATION</title><sec id="caac70016-sec-0150"><title>Intensity&#x02010;modulated and stereotactic radiation therapy</title><p>IMRT and stereotactic RT have become the preferred techniques for&#x000a0;reRT in many scenarios. These approaches allow for precise modulation of high&#x02010;dose regions, enabling clinicians to shift isodose lines away from normal tissue, a major limitation in 3D conformal RT. IMRT, particularly volumetric IMRT, modulates radiation beam intensity precisely to conform closely to the tumor shape, enabling the delivery of high doses while sparing nearby organs. Although no randomized controlled trials have directly compared 3D conformal CRT with IMRT in the reRT setting (Table&#x000a0;<xref rid="caac70016-tbl-0001" ref-type="table">1</xref>), randomized controlled trials in the primary RT setting for HNC have demonstrated that IMRT significantly reduces radiation&#x02010;induced side effects by minimizing exposure to important structures.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0099" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>99</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="caac70016-bib-0100" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>100</sup>
</xref> Supporting its use in reRT, a large, retrospective, multi&#x02010;institutional cohort study of 505 patients with recurrent or second primary HNC demonstrated that IMRT allows for reRT with doses &#x02265;66 Gy to be delivered with curative intent while maintaining an acceptable side&#x02010;effects profile.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0101" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>101</sup>
</xref> IMRT is also particularly valuable for reRT of vertebral metastases in patients with oligometastatic disease, in which sparing the spinal cord is critical,<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0102" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>102</sup>
</xref> as illustrated in Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="caac70016-fig-0007" ref-type="fig">7</xref>.</p><fig position="float" fig-type="FIGURE" id="caac70016-fig-0007"><label>FIGURE 7</label><caption><p>Dosimetric comparison between 3D&#x02010;CRT and IMRT for spinal reRT. (A)&#x000a0;The first&#x02010;course plan (20 Gy in five fractions) delivered using IMRT. (B,C) Second&#x02010;course reRT plans (15 Gy in three fractions) simulated using (B)&#x000a0;3D&#x02010;CRT and (C)&#x000a0;IMRT. (D,E) Cumulative EQD2Gy distributions resulting from the summation of first&#x02010;course and second&#x02010;course plans for (D)&#x000a0;3D&#x02010;CRT (E)&#x000a0;and IMRT, respectively. The dose color wash is standardized across all panels: orange indicates &#x02265;95% of the prescribed dose; yellow, &#x02265;90%; turquoise, &#x02265;80%; royal blue, &#x02265;70%; purple, &#x02265;55%; and dark blue, &#x02265;50%. The spinal cord is contoured in green. Compared with 3D&#x02010;CRT, IMRT achieves a steeper dose gradient and superior sparing of the spinal cord. The cumulative maximum dose to the spinal cord (0.03 cm<sup>3</sup>) reaches 58 Gy EQD2Gy with the IMRT&#x02010;based reRT plan, whereas it exceeds 69 Gy EQD2Gy using the 3D&#x02010;CRT approach. 3D&#x02010;CRT indicates three&#x02010;dimensional conformal radiation therapy; EQD2Gy equivalent dose in 2&#x02010;gray fractions; Gy, grays; IMRT, intensity&#x02010;modulated radiation therapy; reRT, reirradiation; RT, radiation therapy.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="CAAC-75-630-g006" position="anchor" id="jats-graphic-13"/></fig><p>Similarly, stereotactic RT is increasingly used in reRT for small, well defined targets. This approach delivers high doses with submillimeter precision over a limited number of sessions, contrasting with conventional RT, which typically involves many more sessions over several weeks. Stereotactic RT includes stereotactic body RT (SBRT) for targets outside the brain, such as localized tumors in the lungs and liver, and stereotactic radiosurgery or fractionated stereotactic RT for intracranial targets, such as brain tumors. For instance, reRT of spinal metastases has traditionally posed a major challenge because of the risk of spinal cord side effects. SBRT has emerged as a safe and effective option, enabling high&#x02010;dose retreatment with steep dose gradients and limited exposure to surrounding normal tissues. A systematic review of nine studies evaluating SBRT for previously irradiated spinal metastases reported a median 1&#x02010;year local control rate of 76% (range 66%&#x02013;90%), pain relief in 65%&#x02013;81% of patients, and a low incidence of radiation&#x02010;induced myelopathy (1.2%).<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0103" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>103</sup>
</xref> SBRT also plays a major role in the management of peripheral lung recurrences, in which it enables the delivery of tumoricidal doses with a steeper dose fall&#x02010;off around the target while minimizing exposure to surrounding functional lung parenchyma and reducing long&#x02010;term pulmonary side effects (Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="caac70016-fig-0008" ref-type="fig">8</xref>). No randomized controlled trials have directly compared SBRT with other techniques in the reRT setting (Table&#x000a0;<xref rid="caac70016-tbl-0001" ref-type="table">1</xref>). Nevertheless, retrospective studies provide key evidence supporting its role in specific clinical scenarios. For instance, a retrospective multicenter study evaluated 27 patients undergoing a second course of thoracic SBRT with overlap of previously irradiated fields.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0104" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>104</sup>
</xref> Patients received a median dose of 40 Gy in five fractions delivered a median of 20.2 months after the initial treatment. At 1 year, the OS and local control rates were 78.3% and 70.3%, respectively, with no severe side effects reported. In contrast, SBRT remains less effective and carries significant risks when lesions are located near healthy tissue, such as the carotid arteries in HNC.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0105" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>105</sup>
</xref> A study on SBRT for locally recurrent HNC included 46 patients who received a median total dose of 30 Gy over five fractions. Despite achieving good local control (83.8%) and 1&#x02010;year PFS and OS rates of 41% and 46%, respectively, the treatment resulted in significant morbidity.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0106" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>106</sup>
</xref> Long&#x02010;term side effects occurred in 13.3% of patients, and 17.3% experienced carotid blowout syndrome, a fatal complication that developed exclusively in patients who had tumors that surrounded the carotid artery, underscoring the limitations of SBRT in anatomically constrained settings.</p><fig position="float" fig-type="FIGURE" id="caac70016-fig-0008"><label>FIGURE 8</label><caption><p>Dosimetric comparison between IMRT and SBRT for lung reRT. Comparison between (A)&#x000a0;a simulated IMRT plan delivering 50 Gy in 25 fractions and (B)&#x000a0;the clinical SBRT plan delivering 50 Gy in five fractions in a previously irradiated patient with a peripheral lung recurrence. The IMRT plan represents a conventional, homogeneously distributed approach. In contrast, the SBRT plan shows a steeper dose fall&#x02010;off and higher intratumoral dose (up to 107% of the prescription). The high&#x02010;dose volume (orange area, &#x02265;95%) is also smaller with SBRT, because no clinical target volume margin beyond the gross tumor volume was applied. These features illustrate how SBRT may improve the therapeutic ratio in selected reRT scenarios by combining reduced irradiated volume and increased dose conformality. Gy indicates grays; IMRT, intensity&#x02010;modulated radiation therapy; reRT, reirradiation; SBRT, stereotactic body radiation therapy.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="CAAC-75-630-g012" position="anchor" id="jats-graphic-15"/></fig><p>Magnetic resonance&#x02010;guided RT (MRgRT) also represents an innovative advancement, offering the ability to integrate real&#x02010;time MRI with radiation delivery. Modern MRgRT platforms allow for daily, adaptive RT, enabling adjustments to treatment plans based on real&#x02010;time imaging of the patient's anatomy. MRgRT may help further optimize SBRT outcomes in anatomically challenging situations, such as tumors close to essential structures like the spinal cord, esophagus, or large vessels. Preliminary data suggest promising applications in recurrent prostate cancer,<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0107" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>107</sup>
</xref> abdominopelvic malignancies,<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0108" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>108</sup>
</xref> and HNC.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0109" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>109</sup>
</xref> Moreover, biology&#x02010;guided RT is an emerging technique that uses real&#x02010;time PET imaging to refine dose delivery, particularly in reRT settings. In a case of paratracheal lymph node recurrence near the aorta, biology&#x02010;guided reRT reduced the volume of the bronchus receiving high doses by 11% compared with IMRT, potentially lowering the risk of radiation&#x02010;induced bronchial necrosis.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0110" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>110</sup>
</xref>
</p></sec><sec id="caac70016-sec-0160"><title>Brachytherapy</title><p>Brachytherapy is an established technique for reRT. As one of the oldest RT modalities, brachytherapy involves placing radioactive sources directly in or near the tumor, typically using remote after&#x02010;loading of implanted catheters. This approach enables highly localized dose delivery, effectively sparing surrounding tissues while achieving excellent local control. Brachytherapy can be delivered as high&#x02010;dose&#x02010;rate (HDR) therapy, administered over several short treatment fractions, or as low&#x02010;dose&#x02010;rate (LDR) therapy, involving prolonged, continuous exposure over several days. In breast cancer, brachytherapy is a key option for treating ipsilateral breast tumor recurrences. A multicenter, retrospective European study of 508 patients treated with accelerated partial breast reRT after lumpectomy demonstrated excellent oncologic outcomes, with a 5&#x02010;year cumulative incidence of second local relapse of only 4%.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0032" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>32</sup>
</xref> Similarly, in prostate cancer, brachytherapy has demonstrated durable tumor control. A prospective trial of 175 patients treated with HDR brachytherapy for recurrent prostate cancer demonstrated a 3&#x02010;year biochemical recurrence&#x02010;free survival rate of 43% and a local recurrence&#x02010;free survival rate of 51%.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0111" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>111</sup>
</xref> Notably, the only randomized clinical trial directly comparing HDR brachytherapy with external&#x02010;beam RT (EBRT) was conducted in HNC.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0023" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>23</sup>
</xref> In that phase 2 trial, 64 patients with recurrent HNC were randomized to HDR brachytherapy or EBRT. HDR brachytherapy significantly improved 2&#x02010;year local failure&#x02010;free survival (63% vs. 25%; <italic toggle="yes">p</italic>&#x000a0;&#x0003c;&#x000a0;.0001) and 2&#x02010;year OS (67% vs. 32%; <italic toggle="yes">p</italic>&#x000a0;=&#x000a0;0.0002) compared with EBRT. Importantly, severe late side effects were substantially lower with HDR brachytherapy (3.1%) compared with EBRT (35.5%), highlighting its therapeutic advantage in carefully selected patients. HDR interstitial brachytherapy is also a highly effective reRT modality in gynecologic cancers, particularly for central pelvic recurrences involving the vaginal cuff and/or the vulvovaginal region. A retrospective study evaluating HDR brachytherapy in 49 patients who were treated for recurrent gynecologic malignancies reported a favorable 2&#x02010;year OS rate of 51% and a 4&#x02010;year OS rate of 32%.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0112" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>112</sup>
</xref> Despite prior pelvic irradiation, the treatment was well tolerated, with severe late side effects occurring in approximately 10% of patients, underscoring its safety and feasibility. An example of HDR implant placement is illustrated in Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="caac70016-fig-0009" ref-type="fig">9</xref>. Despite its advantages, brachytherapy requires significant technical expertise and specialized infrastructure, which may limit its accessibility in certain centers.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0113" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>113</sup>
</xref> In such settings, SBRT is emerging as a promising alternative because of its logistical feasibility.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0039" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>39</sup>
</xref>
</p><fig position="float" fig-type="FIGURE" id="caac70016-fig-0009"><label>FIGURE 9</label><caption><p>Brachytherapy for central pelvic recurrence after prior radiation therapy. CT scan images in (A)&#x000a0;axial and (B)&#x000a0;coronal views showing the treatment of a central pelvic recurrence using high&#x02010;dose&#x02010;rate brachytherapy in a patient who had already received pelvic RT. The patient, previously treated with surgery and chemoradiation for cervical cancer, developed a local recurrence in the vaginal vault 18 months later. A special applicator was used to place radioactive sources directly into the tumor area. The colored lines represent different dose levels, showing how the radiation is tightly focused on the tumor while sparing nearby healthy tissues. This example illustrates how brachytherapy can safely deliver a second course of RT in selected patients. CT indicates computed tomography; RT, radiation therapy.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="CAAC-75-630-g010" position="anchor" id="jats-graphic-17"/></fig></sec><sec id="caac70016-sec-0170"><title>Intraoperative radiation therapy</title><p>Intraoperative RT (IORT), delivered either as intraoperative electron RT (IOERT) or as HDR intraoperative brachytherapy (HDR&#x02010;IORT), represents another valuable option in selected reRT scenarios, particularly when prior EBRT limits the possibility of delivering tumoricidal doses of reRT without exceeding normal tissue constraints. By delivering a focused radiation boost directly to the tumor bed during surgery after a lower dose of EBRT, IORT improves dose conformity while sparing adjacent healthy tissue. It is most commonly used for pelvic and retroperitoneal recurrences, especially when microscopically complete excision is challenging and/or when tumors are adherent to radiosensitive structures, such as bowel or nerves.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0114" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>114</sup>
</xref> Although no prospective trials have directly compared IORT with external&#x02010;beam reRT in this context, a 2021 meta&#x02010;analysis that included patients with locally advanced or recurrent rectal cancer suggested a potential benefit in local control with IORT (21% recurrences without IORT vs. 15% with IORT; odds ratio, 0.55; <italic toggle="yes">p</italic>&#x000a0;=&#x000a0;0.11).<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0115" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>115</sup>
</xref> Furthermore, a large retrospective study of 158 patients with locally recurrent rectal cancer who were treated after R1 resection compared IOERT (<italic toggle="yes">n</italic>&#x000a0;=&#x000a0;112) versus HDR&#x02010;IORT (<italic toggle="yes">n</italic>&#x000a0;=&#x000a0;46).<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0116" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>116</sup>
</xref> Although OS did not differ significantly (3&#x02010;year OS, 44% for IOERT vs. 39% for HDR&#x02010;IORT; <italic toggle="yes">p</italic>&#x000a0;= 0.747), HDR&#x02010;IORT was associated with significantly improved local recurrence&#x02010;free survival (HR, 0.567; 95% CI, 0.349&#x02013;0.920; <italic toggle="yes">p</italic>&#x000a0;=&#x000a0;0.021). However, this benefit came at the cost of increased major postoperative complications (46% vs. 26%; <italic toggle="yes">p</italic>&#x000a0;=&#x000a0;0.017).</p><p>Beyond the pelvic setting, IORT has also been explored in breast cancer. A multicenter, retrospective Italian study evaluated 109 patients with ipsilateral breast tumor recurrence who underwent a second breast&#x02010;conserving surgery followed by IOERT.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0117" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>117</sup>
</xref> The cumulative incidence of a second local recurrence was 12.2% at 5 years and 32.3% at 10 years. Although OS remained high (88.3% at 10 years), a second local recurrence was associated with a significant decrease in breast cancer&#x02010;specific survival (HR, 9.4; <italic toggle="yes">p</italic>&#x000a0;&#x0003c;&#x000a0;.001). Severe fibrosis (grade &#x02265;3) occurred in 18.9% of patients, and cosmetic outcomes were rated good or excellent in nearly 60% of patients. From a technical perspective, IORT requires close coordination between surgeons and radiation oncologists and the use of specialized equipment, such as shielded operating rooms and/or dedicated mobile radiation devices.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0118" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>118</sup>
</xref> Preoperative imaging helps in planning how and where to deliver the IOERT, and selecting the right patients is essential to ensure that the potential benefit outweighs the risks of surgery.</p><p>Taken together, these data suggest that IORT offers a targeted and potentially effective strategy to improve local control in selected reRT settings&#x02014;particularly when wide surgical margins are difficult to achieve and prior radiation limits external&#x02010;beam delivery to tumoricidal doses. However, its use remains limited to specialized centers and demands strong multidisciplinary collaboration.</p></sec><sec id="caac70016-sec-0180"><title>Proton and ion therapies</title><p>Currently, the majority of global experience with reRT relies on photon&#x02010;based techniques, as previously described. However, proton therapy, and, to a lesser extent, heavier ion therapies (such as carbon and helium ions), have emerged as promising alternatives because of their unique physical and biologic properties.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0119" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>119</sup>
</xref> These charged&#x02010;particle therapies all share the advantageous Bragg peak phenomenon, depositing radiation precisely at the tumor site while significantly reducing exposure to adjacent healthy tissues. Such characteristics make proton therapy particularly attractive in both the adjuvant and definitive reRT settings, especially for recurrent tumors located near critical structures, such as the brainstem, optic pathways, skull base, or major vessels. Carbon ions offer additional advantages through their enhanced biologic effectiveness, particularly in definitive reRT scenarios involving intrinsically radioresistant, recurrent tumors. As discussed above, recurrent cancers often exhibit increased resistance mechanisms, which carbon ions can effectively overcome because of their independence from oxygenation status and specific tumor biology, potentially improving outcomes for challenging recurrent tumors.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0120" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>120</sup>
</xref>
</p><p>Dosimetric (in silico) studies also support the advantages of proton therapy in the reRT setting. For example, a recent multi&#x02010;institutional comparative study evaluated proton versus photon reRT techniques in 24 patients with recurrent lung cancer. Proton therapy provided superior target coverage while significantly decreasing the dose to surrounding normal tissues&#x02014;particularly the spinal cord, esophagus, and lungs&#x02014;compared with photon&#x02010;based techniques, such as IMRT or SBRT.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0121" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>121</sup>
</xref> Similarly, in patients receiving reRT for recurrent breast cancer involving regional lymph nodes, proton therapy achieved better sparing of critical structures, such as the brachial plexus.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0122" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>122</sup>
</xref>
</p><p>Nevertheless, robust clinical evidence confirming these theoretical benefits is still limited. No randomized controlled trials have directly compared proton versus photon therapy in reRT (Table&#x000a0;<xref rid="caac70016-tbl-0001" ref-type="table">1</xref>). Although a few patients who received proton therapy were included in a recent randomized trial on glioblastoma reRT, that study did not formally compare outcomes between modalities.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0029" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>29</sup>
</xref> Therefore, most of the existing evidence supporting proton reRT comes from noncomparative, retrospective studies&#x02014;including some large, single&#x02010;institution cohorts&#x02014;as well as small prospective series. Designing randomized trials in this context poses significant challenges, such as limited eligible patient populations, unequal access to proton therapy facilities, and lack of equipoise among both physicians and patients. These obstacles have been well documented even in the de novo setting and are further amplified in the reRT context. Available data primarily concern tumors of the central nervous system, head and neck, lung, and, to a lesser extent, gastrointestinal (GI) malignancies.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0011" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>11</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="caac70016-bib-0123" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>123</sup>
</xref> In HNC, a retrospective cohort of 242 patients with recurrent or second primary HNC treated with proton reRT reported a 1&#x02010;year local control rate of 71.8% and a 1&#x02010;year OS rate of 66.6%.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0124" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>124</sup>
</xref> These outcomes appear favorable compared with historical series that used photon&#x02010;based reRT, although no direct comparison was performed. Similarly, in recurrent glioblastoma, a retrospective analysis of 78 patients demonstrated improved median OS with carbon&#x02010;ion therapy versus photon&#x02010;based stereotactic RT (8.0 vs. 6.5 months; <italic toggle="yes">p</italic>&#x000a0;=&#x000a0;0.046), with a 1&#x02010;year OS rate of 29% versus 10%, respectively, without increasing side effects.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0125" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>125</sup>
</xref> After propensity score matching, this survival advantage persisted (median OS, 8.9 vs. 7.2 months; <italic toggle="yes">p</italic>&#x000a0;=&#x000a0;0.041). In addition, a retrospective study on particle therapy (protons or carbon ions) for head and neck reRT indicated a risk of 2.7% for carotid artery rupture, which lies on the lower edge of the spectrum for this severe complication reported after treatment.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0126" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>126</sup>
</xref>
</p><p>The specificities of proton therapy planning should be carefully considered. Proton beams are particularly sensitive to uncertainties related to patient positioning; anatomic variations, such as air cavities or fluid shifts; and tissue density heterogeneities, which can significantly affect range accuracy. In addition, variation in linear energy transfer along the proton beam path can affect its biologic effectiveness, introducing further complexity into treatment planning.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0127" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>127</sup>
</xref> This is especially relevant when recurrent tumors are located next to critical normal tissue (e.g., brainstem, optic nerves) because the increase in linear energy transfer at the distal edge of the proton range may lead to a higher&#x02010;than&#x02010;expected biologic effect on surrounding normal tissues.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0128" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>128</sup>
</xref> To manage these technical challenges, proton therapy planning requires robust optimization methods, meticulous imaging protocols, and frequent adaptive replanning throughout the treatment course.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0129" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>129</sup>
</xref> Moreover, global accessibility remains limited because of higher infrastructure costs and fewer treatment facilities compared with conventional RT.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0011" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>11</sup>
</xref> Consequently, patient selection for proton reRT often depends on logistical considerations, institutional guidelines, and insurance coverage rather than clearly defined, evidence&#x02010;based criteria, resulting in considerable international variation in clinical practices.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0130" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>130</sup>
</xref>
</p></sec><sec id="caac70016-sec-0190"><title>Hyperthermia</title><p>A valuable adjunct to reRT, hyperthermia is particularly useful for superficial recurrences of breast cancer. By raising tissue temperatures, hyperthermia enhances radiation sensitivity through mechanisms such as impaired DNA repair, reduced tumor hypoxia, and increased blood flow, leading to improved tumor response and local control. A systematic review and meta&#x02010;analysis of 34 studies, including both single&#x02010;arm and randomized trials, demonstrated the significant benefit of hyperthermia in this setting.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0131" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>131</sup>
</xref> In comparative trials comparing reRT alone versus reRT plus hyperthermia, complete response rates were markedly higher with hyperthermia (60.2% vs. 38.1%; <italic toggle="yes">p</italic>&#x000a0;&#x0003c;&#x000a0;.0001). Similarly, 26 single&#x02010;arm studies reported a complete response rate of 63.4%, including 66.6% for previously irradiated patients. Importantly, severe late side effects were infrequent, with a reported rate of 5.2%, highlighting the safety and efficacy of hyperthermia as an adjunct to reRT for superficial recurrences. This radiosensitizing effect of hyperthermia was further confirmed in a recent historical cohort study of 112 patients with locoregional recurrent breast cancer who received postoperative reRT and hyperthermia.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0132" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>132</sup>
</xref> That study demonstrated a strong, continuous thermal dose&#x02010;effect relationship: a twofold increase in thermal dose was associated with a 5%&#x02013;10% improvement in 5&#x02010;year locoregional control and a 48% reduction in the risk of recurrence (<italic toggle="yes">p</italic>&#x000a0;=&#x000a0;0.001).</p></sec><sec id="caac70016-sec-0200"><title>FLASH RT</title><p>A cutting&#x02010;edge approach currently under investigation is FLASH RT, which delivers ultrahigh dose rates over a fraction of a second. This technology has shown promise in preclinical studies, with evidence suggesting that it may widen the therapeutic window by reducing normal tissue side effects while maintaining effective tumor control, a potential benefit in reRT.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0133" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>133</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="caac70016-bib-0134" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>134</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="caac70016-bib-0135" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>135</sup>
</xref> However, significant uncertainties remain, particularly regarding the translation of these benefits to clinically relevant settings. Current limitations include the lack of data on larger treatment volumes and fractionated schedules that reflect real&#x02010;world clinical scenarios as well as the need to confirm the safety and efficacy of FLASH RT near sensitive structures. Although FLASH RT holds potential for challenging indications, such as reRT for head and neck,<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0136" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>136</sup>
</xref> brain, and lung cancers, in which dose escalation is constrained, it remains experimental and requires further clinical validation.</p></sec></sec><sec id="caac70016-sec-0210"><title>SITE&#x02010;SPECIFIC CONSIDERATIONS</title><sec id="caac70016-sec-0220"><title>Head and neck cancer</title><p>For patients who have HNC with locoregional recurrence after initial RT, reRT can be administered with curative intent but presents challenges related to prior treatment side effects, comorbidities, and planning around cumulative dose constraints. Key risks include severe side effects, such as dysphagia, carotid artery rupture, or brain necrosis, particularly near high&#x02010;risk structures.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0014" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>14</sup>
</xref>
</p><p>ReRT should generally be avoided in patients who have experienced any prior radiation&#x02010;induced side effects affecting critical structures, such as the brainstem, spinal cord, or optic chiasm, as well as those with significant organ dysfunction or severe comorbidities.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0043" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>43</sup>
</xref> In addition, although most published reRT series demonstrate that a second RT course 6 months after initial treatment is feasible, consensus opinion holds that an interval of at least 12 months between the initial and reRT courses is best to mitigate risks and is associated with improved outcomes.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0043" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>43</sup>
</xref> For postoperative reRT, treatment is typically reserved for individuals with positive surgical margins, or margins of &#x0003c;1 mm, or extracapsular extension of nodal metastases to enhance disease control potential.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0137" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>137</sup>
</xref>
</p><p>Advancements in imaging technology significantly enhance the precision of target volume delineation for head and neck reRT by providing detailed visualization of tumor boundaries and adjacent critical structures. MRI is particularly valuable for defining tumor margins near the skull base, where soft tissue contrast is critical for distinguishing tumors from normal anatomy. For nonskull base recurrences, dual&#x02010;energy computed tomography (CT) offers improved tissue characterization, aiding in precise contouring.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0138" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>138</sup>
</xref>
<sup>18</sup>F&#x02010;fluorodeoxyglucose (<sup>18</sup>F&#x02010;FDG) PET imaging further refines target volumes by identifying areas of high metabolic activity, which may correlate with aggressive tumor behavior. This information allows clinicians to adapt treatment plans by potentially intensifying the dose to regions with a high risk of recurrence while sparing less aggressive areas, thus balancing efficacy and side effects.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0139" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>139</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="caac70016-bib-0140" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>140</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="caac70016-bib-0141" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>141</sup>
</xref> These imaging modalities collectively ensure that target volumes are both anatomically accurate and biologically informed, improving outcomes and reducing the risk of side effects.</p><p>Target volumes are carefully minimized to spare healthy tissues and reduce adverse effects. The GTV is typically expanded by 5&#x02013;10 mm to create the CTV. This expansion accounts for potential microscopic tumor spread, with specific anatomic adjustments made to avoid over&#x02010;irradiating structures such as air cavities and the skull base.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0043" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>43</sup>
</xref> In postoperative reRT, the CTV is designed to include the tumor bed with an additional 5&#x02010;mm margin to address any microscopic residual disease.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0137" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>137</sup>
</xref> Irradiation to elective lymph nodes is generally avoided to limit side effects and preserve healthy tissue function.</p><p>IMRT remains the standard approach for HNC reRT, providing high conformality and the ability to limit dose exposure to sensitive organs. However, more than one half of patients face either a second recurrence or mortality within 2 years after IMRT&#x02010;based reRT.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0013" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>13</sup>
</xref> For patients with smaller tumor volumes and minimal preexisting organ dysfunction, SBRT presents a viable alternative,<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0142" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>142</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="caac70016-bib-0143" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>143</sup>
</xref> achieving outcomes similar to those using IMRT (2&#x02010;year local control of 48% and OS of 30%) with fewer sessions and lower side effects (severe side effects at 9.6%).<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0144" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>144</sup>
</xref> Nonetheless, SBRT is not without risks: carotid blowout is a significant concern in patients with recurrent HNC, particularly when tumors invade or encase the carotid artery or when cumulative radiation doses are high.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0061" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>61</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="caac70016-bib-0105" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>105</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="caac70016-bib-0143" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>143</sup>
</xref> Emerging techniques like proton therapy (Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="caac70016-fig-0010" ref-type="fig">10</xref>) and carbon&#x02010;ion reRT are showing promise in managing recurrent HNC while potentially reducing side effects.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0145" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>145</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="caac70016-bib-0146" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>146</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="caac70016-bib-0147" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>147</sup>
</xref> Proton therapy has demonstrated 1&#x02010;year local control and OS rates of 71.8% and 66.6%, respectively, with a 36% incidence of severe side effects, including five treatment&#x02010;associated deaths.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0124" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>124</sup>
</xref> Carbon&#x02010;ion RT offers a notable advancement, with a 1&#x02010;year OS rate of 81.5% and a low incidence of severe side effects, including a carotid blowout rate of only 2.7%, suggesting its suitability for patients with challenging anatomy.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0126" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>126</sup>
</xref>
</p><fig position="float" fig-type="FIGURE" id="caac70016-fig-0010"><label>FIGURE 10</label><caption><p>Comparison of proton therapy and IMRT for reRT of a patient with recurrent nasopharyngeal cancer. Dose distributions for (A)&#x000a0;proton therapy and (B)&#x000a0;IMRT are compared. Warmer colors represent higher doses, and cooler colors represent lower doses. In A, proton therapy exhibits superior sparing of critical structures, particularly the brain (teal), brainstem (orange), and parotids (green). Gy indicates grays; IMRT, intensity&#x02010;modulated radiation therapy; reRT, reirradiation.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="CAAC-75-630-g011" position="anchor" id="jats-graphic-19"/></fig></sec><sec id="caac70016-sec-0230"><title>Brain tumors</title><p>For individuals experiencing local recurrence of brain tumors after an initial course of RT, reRT is an option for either curative or palliative intent.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0008" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>8</sup>
</xref> However, this approach carries significant risks, including brain necrosis, cognitive impairment, and vision loss, necessitating careful patient selection. ReRT is typically offered to patients with good PS,<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0148" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>148</sup>
</xref> small recurrent tumor volumes, prior surgery, and the potential to benefit from concurrent therapies that may enhance survival outcomes.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0149" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>149</sup>
</xref> A minimum interval of 6 months between the initial and the reRT is recommended to allow for radiobiologic recovery,<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0150" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>150</sup>
</xref> with better survival outcomes observed when this interval exceeds 29 months.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0151" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>151</sup>
</xref> To further refine patient selection, the Deutsches Konsortium f&#x000fc;r Translationale Krebsforschung/Radiation Oncology Group analysis validated a prognostic score specific to brain cancers&#x02014;the reirradiation risk score&#x02014;based on clinical performance, age, and initial histology.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0152" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>152</sup>
</xref> In the development cohort of 353 patients, the reirradiation risk score stratified patients into three risk categories with median postrecurrence survival of 14.2, 9.1, and 5.3 months, respectively. External validation in 212 patients confirmed the tool's predictive accuracy, yielding median postrecurrence survival of 13.8, 8.8, and 3.8 months for the same categories.</p><p>In reRT planning for brain tumors, advanced imaging modalities are essential for accurate target delineation. Thin&#x02010;slice MRI is the primary choice, often complemented by simulation CT. Certain MRI sequences, such as spectroscopy and PET imaging with specific tracers, can further enhance precision.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0153" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>153</sup>
</xref> For instance, combining MRI with carbon&#x02010;11 methionine PET has improved accuracy in detecting tumor extensions in recurrent glioblastoma.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0154" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>154</sup>
</xref>
<sup>18</sup>F&#x02013;fluoroethyl&#x02010;L&#x02010;tyrosine PET alongside gadolinium contrast&#x02010;enhanced, T1&#x02010;weighted MRI offers a more comprehensive understanding of tumor boundaries,<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0155" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>155</sup>
</xref> whereas <sup>18</sup>F&#x02010;DOPA PET has shown superiority over contrast&#x02010;enhanced MRI in detecting biopsy&#x02010;confirmed, recurrent glioblastoma.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0156" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>156</sup>
</xref> Spectroscopic MRI also offers additional insights, often revealing larger tumor volumes using choline/N&#x02010;acetylaspartate ratios.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0157" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>157</sup>
</xref> However, contemporary trials, including the multicenter GLIAA trial (ClinicalTrials.gov identifier NCT01252459), found no significant difference in PFS, OS, or local control between reRT guided by <sup>18</sup>F&#x02010;fluoroethyl&#x02010;L&#x02010;tyrosine PET and gadolinium contrast&#x02010;enhanced, T1&#x02010;weighted MRI, suggesting that both imaging approaches are valid for reRT planning.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0158" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>158</sup>
</xref>
</p><p>The lack of consensus for target volume delineation in glioblastoma reRT has led to a patient&#x02010;centered approach focused on minimizing healthy tissue exposure.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0044" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>44</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="caac70016-bib-0045" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>45</sup>
</xref> Standard practice emphasizes keeping target volumes as small as possible. This includes using the recurrent GTV with minimal CTV expansion, often limited to 3 mm to avoid unnecessary exposure to surrounding healthy brain tissues.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0044" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>44</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="caac70016-bib-0159" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>159</sup>
</xref> However, newer RT strategies with differential sparing of normal tissue (larger therapeutic window) might allow for safe inclusion of larger volumes of potential microscopic spread in treatment targets. For example, this might be the case for pulsed, reduced&#x02010;dose&#x02010;rate reRT, in which the prescribed dose of radiation is administered in multiple subfractions at specific time intervals within a single treatment session. In this setting, some groups have advocated for inclusion of not only the contrast&#x02010;enhancing lesion but also areas of FLAIR (fluid&#x02010;attenuated inversion recovery) hyperintensity.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0160" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>160</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="caac70016-bib-0161" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>161</sup>
</xref>
</p><p>High&#x02010;precision techniques are essential in brain reRT because of the proximity of essential structures, such as the optic nerves and brainstem. Stereotactic radiosurgery and fractionated stereotactic RT are preferred options for delivering targeted, high&#x02010;dose radiation with millimeter&#x02010;level precision (Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="caac70016-fig-0011" ref-type="fig">11</xref>). In the NRG Oncology/Radiation Therapy Oncology Group 1205 trial, hypofractionated stereotactic RT combined with bevacizumab achieved a 6&#x02010;month PFS rate of 54%, nearly doubling the PFS compared with bevacizumab alone (54.3% vs. 29.1%; <italic toggle="yes">p</italic>&#x000a0;=&#x000a0;0.001), although the median OS remained similar (10.1 vs. 9.7 months, respectively; <italic toggle="yes">p</italic>&#x000a0;=&#x000a0;0.46). Side effects were more frequent in the reRT arm, with treatment&#x02010;related deaths occurring in 9.6% of patients versus 5.3% of those in the bevacizumab&#x02010;only group.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0029" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>29</sup>
</xref> Combining stereotactic RT with systemic treatments, such as APG101 or other agents, has shown additional potential to improve PFS and OS in recurrent glioblastoma, highlighting the importance of multimodal approaches in optimizing outcomes.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0026" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>26</sup>
</xref>
</p><fig position="float" fig-type="FIGURE" id="caac70016-fig-0011"><label>FIGURE 11</label><caption><p>ReRT plan for a patient with recurrent glioblastoma. (A)&#x000a0;Initial CT scan and treatment plan for the first irradiation. (B)&#x000a0;Gadolinium&#x02010;enhanced T1 MRI revealing an in&#x02010;field recurrence 8 months after the initial RT, with a color wash representing the stereotactic RT reRT plan. The reRT volume and technique were carefully optimized to minimize the dose to healthy brain tissue. CT indicates computed tomography; MRI, magnetic resonance imaging; reRT, reirradiation; RT, radiation therapy.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="CAAC-75-630-g002" position="anchor" id="jats-graphic-21"/></fig></sec><sec id="caac70016-sec-0240"><title>Lung cancer</title><p>The main risks associated with lung reRT vary, depending on whether or not the recurrence is located in the vicinity of mediastinal structures, i.e., in a central or peripheral location. For centrally located recurrences, high&#x02010;dose reRT may pose significant risks to mediastinal structures, including the esophagus (leading to stenosis, fistula, or bleeding) and the bronchial tree (with similar risks of stenosis or fistula formation). Peripheral lung recurrences can increase the risk of pneumonitis, with additional considerations for the brachial plexus and the chest wall with intercostal neuropathic pain or rib fracture.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0162" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>162</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="caac70016-bib-0163" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>163</sup>
</xref> The latter are frequently asymptomatic and should not be prohibitive for reRT. To guide risk assessment, Liu et&#x000a0;al. proposed a scoring system to predict the likelihood of severe radiation pneumonitis after lung reRT.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0164" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>164</sup>
</xref> This model includes both clinical factors, such as poor general condition (ECOG PS 2&#x02013;3) and reduced lung function before reRT, dosimetric parameters, and prior RT to both sides of the mediastinum. Higher scores were associated with a significantly increased risk of serious lung side effects, providing a practical tool to help identify patients at higher risk before considering reRT. Patients with an ECOG PS of 0&#x02013;2 are generally considered suitable for reRT. In selected cases, patients with a PS of 3 also may be eligible for SBRT&#x02010;based reRT if their PS decline is caused by nonrespiratory factors.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0042" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>42</sup>
</xref> Importantly, reRT should not be recommended for patients with interstitial lung disease.</p><p>Effective imaging for reRT planning includes CT scans to map tumor and organ anatomy. MRI may be necessary to rule out invasion of mediastinal structures or the brachial plexus.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0042" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>42</sup>
</xref>
<sup>18</sup>F&#x02010;FDG PET imaging can help differentiate between fibrosis and progressive tumor on thoracic CT, supporting a precise delineation of tumor boundaries (Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="caac70016-fig-0012" ref-type="fig">12</xref>).</p><fig position="float" fig-type="FIGURE" id="caac70016-fig-0012"><label>FIGURE 12</label><caption><p>Differentiating radiation&#x02010;induced fibrosis from local recurrence in a patient aged 62 years who was previously irradiated for right upper lobe pulmonary adenocarcinoma. (A)&#x000a0;Serial CT scans showed progressive opacifications, which were indeterminate for fibrosis or recurrence. (B)&#x000a0;<sup>18</sup>F&#x02010;FDG PET imaging confirmed the recurrence. (C)&#x000a0;SBRT plan with PET&#x02010;guided targeting to ensure precise reRT while sparing fibrotic lung tissue. <sup>18</sup>F&#x02010;FDG&#x000a0;indicates fluorine&#x02010;18&#x02013;labeled fluorodeoxyglucose; CT, computed tomography; PET, positron emission tomography; reRT, reirradiation; SBRT, stereotactic body radiation therapy.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="CAAC-75-630-g007" position="anchor" id="jats-graphic-23"/></fig><p>The target volume in reRT is kept as small as possible to minimize healthy tissue exposure. In most cases, this includes only the GTV.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0165" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>165</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="caac70016-bib-0166" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>166</sup>
</xref> For lung lesions, an internal target volume is typically added to account for respiratory motion.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0162" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>162</sup>
</xref> Elective nodal irradiation is generally avoided. For cases in which there is uncertainty in GTV delineation or suspected microscopic infiltration, selective CTV use may be appropriate.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0057" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>57</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="caac70016-bib-0163" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>163</sup>
</xref>
</p><p>Although no randomized clinical trials have been published, retrospective studies provide insights into the efficacy and safety of various thoracic reRT approaches. SBRT has proven to be highly effective in controlling peripheral nonsmall cell lung cancer (NSCLC) recurrences, with a lower risk of side effects compared with centrally located lesions. In a retrospective study involving 36 patients who were previously treated with thoracic RT and subsequently received reRT with SBRT for peripheral NSCLC, the 2&#x02010;year OS rate was 59%, and the local control rate reached 92%.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0057" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>57</sup>
</xref> Treatment&#x02010;related side effects were mostly moderate and manageable, with 50% of patients reporting worsening dyspnea, 30% experiencing chest wall pain, and 8% developing significant esophagitis. No severe side effects or treatment&#x02010;related deaths were observed. For centrally located recurrences, SBRT presents greater challenges because of the proximity of mediastinal structures. In a study involving 33 patients who received SBRT for central NSCLC recurrences, the 2&#x02010;year OS rate was 45%, and the local control rate was 67%.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0162" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>162</sup>
</xref> However, severe side effects were frequent, including chest wall pain, dyspnea, and one fatal aorta&#x02010;esophageal fistula. Another study of 39 patients who received SBRT for recurrent or secondary lung tumors, including both central and peripheral lesions, reported 1&#x02010;year and 2&#x02010;year local control rates of 77% and 64%, respectively. That study highlighted findings that smaller tumor volumes, peripheral lesion location, and a longer interval since the initial radiation course were associated with improved OS and local failure&#x02010;free survival, underscoring the significance of patient&#x02010;specific and tumor&#x02010;specific factors in achieving favorable outcomes.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0167" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>167</sup>
</xref> Proton therapy represents another option for reRT in NSCLC. In a multicenter prospective trial of patients with recurrent NSCLC located near prior radiation fields, the 1&#x02010;year OS and PFS rates were 59% and 58%, respectively.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0168" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>168</sup>
</xref> However, significant side effects occurred, with 42% of patients experiencing serious side effects, including six treatment&#x02010;related deaths primarily linked to large target volumes and proximity to important structures, such as the esophagus and heart. Carbon&#x02010;ion RT is another potential treatment modality that has shown favorable outcomes in patients with locoregionally recurrent or metastatic lung tumors. In a retrospective study involving 95 patients, the 2&#x02010;year OS rate was 61.9%, and the local control rate reached 54%.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0169" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>169</sup>
</xref> Severe side effects were relatively rare, with one treatment&#x02010;related death from bronchopleural fistula. These results suggest that carbon&#x02010;ion RT may offer effective local control with a favorable safety profile. Further trials are needed to confirm its benefits compared with photon&#x02010;based techniques.</p></sec><sec id="caac70016-sec-0250"><title>Prostate cancer</title><p>Prostate reRT is typically considered for patients with localized intraprostatic recurrence after an initial RT course. This patient group often faces significant risks of genitourinary (GU) and GI side effects, with fistula formation and hemorrhage being key considerations in reRT.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0046" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>46</sup>
</xref>
</p><p>Candidates for reRT typically undergo baseline tests, including prostate&#x02010;specific antigen levels, multiparametric MRI, and prostate&#x02010;specific membrane antigen PET, with biopsy confirmation being strongly recommended. An interval of 2&#x02013;3 years between initial RT and reRT is advised, with a prostate&#x02010;specific antigen doubling time greater than 6 months favoring better outcomes.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0047" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>47</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="caac70016-bib-0170" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>170</sup>
</xref> In addition, baseline GU function is evaluated using the IPSS, with scores limited to approximately 15 for salvage candidates. Patients with moderate&#x02010;to&#x02010;severe late GU or GI side effects after previous RT are typically excluded from reRT consideration because of the increased risk of severe side effects.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0171" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>171</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="caac70016-bib-0172" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>172</sup>
</xref>
</p><p>For imaging, multiparametric MRI remains the standard to localize intraprostatic recurrence, despite some limitations in underestimating tumor extent compared with histopathology.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0173" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>173</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="caac70016-bib-0174" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>174</sup>
</xref> A structured reporting approach has been proposed for MRI to standardize recurrence assessment, including five&#x02010;point scales for diffusion&#x02010;weighted imaging and dynamic contrast&#x02010;enhanced sequences, with lesion proximity to the initial tumor location being a critical planning factor.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0175" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>175</sup>
</xref> Although prostate&#x02010;specific membrane antigen PET is recommended for pelvic and metastatic disease assessment, its role in precise intraprostatic tumor delineation remains under investigation.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0176" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>176</sup>
</xref>
</p><p>Delineation strategies for prostate reRT typically involve either whole&#x02010;gland or focal salvage approaches, selected based on individual clinical scenarios.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0170" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>170</sup>
</xref> For focal salvage treatments, it is recommended to define the GTV on multiparametric MRI (Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="caac70016-fig-0013" ref-type="fig">13</xref>), expand it by 3&#x02013;5 mm, and trim it to the prostate contour to create the CTV, ensuring accurate targeting while sparing surrounding tissues.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0171" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>171</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="caac70016-bib-0172" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>172</sup>
</xref>
</p><fig position="float" fig-type="FIGURE" id="caac70016-fig-0013"><label>FIGURE 13</label><caption><p>Multiparametric MRI for SBRT reRT planning in a patient with recurrent prostate cancer. A patient aged 70 years with a history of prostate cancer who was previously treated with RT and hormone therapy presented with a PSA rise to 2.07 ng/mL. Multiparametric MRI identified a 14&#x02010;mm recurrent lesion, which was confirmed as invasive adenocarcinoma (Gleason score 8) by biopsy. (A)&#x000a0;CT simulation scan with the patient's GTV outlined in red. (B)&#x000a0;Multiparametric MRI in the treatment position, enhancing precise GTV delineation for SBRT. CT indicates computed tomography; GTV, gross tumor volume; MRI, magnetic resonance imaging; PSA, prostate&#x02010;specific antigen; RT, radiation therapy; SBRT, stereotactic body radiation therapy.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="CAAC-75-630-g005" position="anchor" id="jats-graphic-25"/></fig><p>Despite the absence of randomized clinical trials directly comparing SBRT with brachytherapy for prostate reRT, both approaches are widely used, and studies suggest comparable efficacy.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0171" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>171</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="caac70016-bib-0177" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>177</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="caac70016-bib-0178" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>178</sup>
</xref> A systematic review, which included 150 studies&#x02014;the majority of which were retrospective&#x02014;reporting on 11,322 patients, quantitatively analyzed outcomes across various treatments for recurrent prostate cancer.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0039" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>39</sup>
</xref> These treatments included radical prostatectomy (52 studies), cryotherapy (32 studies), high&#x02010;intensity focused ultrasound (20 studies), SBRT (eight studies), HDR brachytherapy (16 studies), and LDR brachytherapy (32 studies). For brachytherapy, relapse&#x02010;free survival rates were approximately 60% for HDR and 56% for LDR, with severe GU side effects reported in 8% of patients and minimal severe GI side effects (0% for HDR and 1.5% for LDR). These favorable outcomes make brachytherapy an attractive option compared with more invasive methods like prostatectomy, high&#x02010;intensity focused ultrasound, and cryotherapy. For SBRT protocols, biochemical relapse&#x02010;free survival rates reach 59.3% with an average follow&#x02010;up of 26 months. The incidence of late severe GU side effects is approximately 3.4%, whereas the incidence of GI side effects is approximately 2%.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0179" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>179</sup>
</xref> Improved biochemical relapse&#x02010;free survival and local control have been observed with dose escalation, although this also increases the risk of GU and GI side effects. Partial prostate irradiation with SBRT has shown reduced GU/GI side effects while preserving effective local control, underscoring its value in minimizing adverse effects for selected reirradiated patients.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0179" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>179</sup>
</xref> Currently, there is a randomized trial comparing SBRT with brachytherapy (RO&#x02010;PIP) running in the United Kingdom (ISRCTN [International Standard Randomised Controlled Trial Number] 12238218).<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0180" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>180</sup>
</xref>
</p></sec><sec id="caac70016-sec-0260"><title>Rectal cancer</title><p>ReRT for locally recurrent rectal cancer is usually considered either as a preoperative treatment, to facilitate R0 or R1 resection, or as a definitive treatment option for nonoperable patients. It carries a substantial risk of GI side effects, particularly involving the small bowel, rectum, bladder, and sacral plexus.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0049" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>49</sup>
</xref> Severe late side effects&#x02014;including fistula formation, small bowel obstruction, and presacral abscess&#x02014;have been reported in up to 34% of patients, with the risk increasing significantly when reRT is combined with surgery (HR, 2.96; 95% CI, 1.30&#x02013;7.32; <italic toggle="yes">p</italic>&#x000a0;=&#x000a0;0.012).<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0181" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>181</sup>
</xref>&#x000a0;Despite these risks, the significant effect of uncontrolled locally recurrent rectal cancer on quality of life&#x02014;because of pain, discharge, or pelvic organ dysfunction&#x02014;often justifies the acceptance of substantial side&#x02010;effect risks in an effort to achieve local control.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0182" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>182</sup>
</xref> A shorter interval between initial RT and reRT, particularly less than 24 months, has been associated with increased side&#x02010;effect rates.</p><p>Candidates for reRT typically present with a good performance status (ECOG 0&#x02013;2), no distant metastases, and an interval of at least 6&#x02013;12 months since prior pelvic RT.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0050" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>50</sup>
</xref> Therefore, the decision to proceed with reRT must consider prior treatments, anatomic constraints, and the risk of cumulative side effects.</p><p>Imaging&#x02010;based delineation of recurrence can be particularly challenging in locally recurrent rectal cancer because of postoperative anatomic distortion, fibrosis, and chronic inflammation.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0085" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>85</sup>
</xref> Multiparametric pelvic MRI remains the gold standard for local assessment, with diffusion&#x02010;weighted imaging helping to distinguish viable tumor from fibrosis (Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="caac70016-fig-0003" ref-type="fig">3</xref>).<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0183" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>183</sup>
</xref>
<sup>18</sup>F&#x02010;FDG PET can complement MRI, especially in cases of diagnostic uncertainty, such as rising carcinoembryonic antigen levels with inconclusive CT imaging,<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0184" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>184</sup>
</xref> although its sensitivity may be limited in mucinous tumors.</p><p>Delineation of the GTV should rely on T2&#x02010;weighted and diffusion&#x02010;weighted MRI sequences. Consensus guidelines from the PelvEx II trial (ClinicalTrials.gov identifier NCT04389086) recommend defining the CTV expanding the GTV with a 1.0&#x02013;1.5 cm margin, respecting anatomic boundaries, and adapting to postoperative changes.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0185" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>185</sup>
</xref> MRI acquisition should avoid angulation and include the entire pelvis to ensure reproducibility.</p><p>Among external&#x02010;beam techniques, hyperfractionated chemotherapy&#x02010;reRT&#x02014;delivering two smaller fractions daily&#x02014;is one of the most studied in this setting. A prospective, multicenter phase 2 trial enrolled 59 patients with locally recurrent rectal cancer who were previously treated with pelvic RT. Hyperfractionated reRT combined with continuous&#x02010;infusion 5&#x02010;fluorouracil achieved a complete resection in 36% of patients and a 5&#x02010;year OS rate of 39%.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0186" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>186</sup>
</xref> These findings were echoed in a retrospective cohort of 102 patients, reporting 3&#x02010;year local control and OS rates of 40% and 39%, respectively.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0181" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>181</sup>
</xref> In addition, brachytherapy has shown promise in select cases. A retrospective series of nine patients treated with HDR interstitial brachytherapy, either alone or in combination with EBRT, reported an 8&#x02010;year OS rate of 56% and a local control rate of 44%.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0187" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>187</sup>
</xref> Severe side effects occurred in three patients who had large tumors or invasion of adjacent organs, underscoring the need for cautious selection. IORT, delivered as IOERT or HDR intraoperative brachytherapy (HDR&#x02010;IORT), is also used in locally recurrent rectal cancer&#x02014;particularly after microscopically incomplete resections. A 2021 meta&#x02010;analysis suggested improved local control with IORT (15% recurrence with IORT vs. 21% without IORT; OR, 0.55; <italic toggle="yes">p</italic>&#x000a0;=&#x000a0;0.11), although no survival benefit was observed.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0115" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>115</sup>
</xref> Proton therapy has also been evaluated for reRT in recurrent rectal cancer: in a retrospective series that included 15 patients&#x02014;of whom six had rectal cancer&#x02014;treated with hyperfractionated proton reRT after a median interval of 4.7 years, no late side effects were observed, and only mild acute side effects were reported (grade 1 pain, diarrhea, or fatigue).<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0188" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>188</sup>
</xref>
</p></sec><sec id="caac70016-sec-0270"><title>Gynecologic cancer</title><p>ReRT for recurrent gynecologic cancers carries a substantial risk of GI and GU side effects, particularly involving the rectum, bladder, ureters, and vaginal wall. Severe late side effects, such as fistula formation, tissue necrosis, cystitis, or vaginal ulcers, have been observed in approximately 15%&#x02013;25% of patients, especially when standard organ dose constraints are intentionally exceeded.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0004" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>4</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="caac70016-bib-0052" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>52</sup>
</xref> This risk increases significantly in patients with larger recurrent tumors or those who are reirradiated within short intervals after initial RT.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0052" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>52</sup>
</xref>
</p><p>Candidates most likely to benefit from reRT typically have a good performance status (ECOG 0&#x02013;1), centrally located pelvic recurrences, and no evidence of distant metastases.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0004" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>4</sup>
</xref> In addition, a longer interval between radiation courses (ideally more than 6 months) and smaller tumor diameter (&#x0003c;4 cm) have been associated with better survival outcomes.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0051" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>51</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="caac70016-bib-0052" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>52</sup>
</xref>
</p><p>Imaging&#x02010;based delineation of recurrence in gynecologic cancers can be particularly difficult because of posttreatment anatomic distortions. Radiation&#x02010;induced changes, such as uterine atrophy, loss of the junctional zone, cervical fibrosis, and ovarian shrinkage, can mimic tumor recurrence, especially in premenopausal women.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0086" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>86</sup>
</xref> Multiparametric pelvic MRI is the imaging modality of choice, often supplemented by diffusion&#x02010;weighted imaging and contrast&#x02010;enhanced sequences to help distinguish between fibrosis and viable tumor tissue. In addition, late complications, such as fistulas between the bladder, rectum, and vagina or radiation&#x02010;induced enteritis and cystitis, can further complicate interpretation. In such patients, MRI, particularly in the sagittal plane and using heavily T2&#x02010;weighted or postcontrast sequences, can help distinguish fistulous tracts from recurrent malignancy.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0086" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>86</sup>
</xref>
</p><p>GTV delineation should rely primarily on T2&#x02010;weighted and diffusion&#x02010;weighted MRI sequences to accurately differentiate tumor recurrence from radiation fibrosis.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0004" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>4</sup>
</xref> For central pelvic recurrences, the CTV typically includes the entire vaginal vault and paravaginal tissues, adjusted to account for prior dose distribution and anatomic shifts posttreatment.</p><p>HDR interstitial brachytherapy is a commonly used technique for central pelvic recurrences (Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="caac70016-fig-0009" ref-type="fig">9</xref>). A retrospective study of 52 patients with recurrent cervical cancer who received salvage HDR interstitial brachytherapy demonstrated encouraging outcomes, with a 3&#x02010;year local control rate of 61.5% and a 3&#x02010;year OS rate of 62.3%.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0052" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>52</sup>
</xref> Similar findings were reported in a larger retrospective cohort involving 76 patients who received HDR brachytherapy reRT with various applicators. At 2 years, the local control and OS rates were 60% and 72.9%, respectively, highlighting the effectiveness of this approach.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0189" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>189</sup>
</xref> However, severe late toxicities were observed, including rectal side effects (13%), bladder side effects (8%), and vaginal fibrosis (31%), underscoring the need for careful patient selection and precise treatment planning. For recurrences at the pelvic sidewall or not amenable to brachytherapy, SBRT represents a feasible alternative. In a retrospective study of 23 patients with pelvic sidewall cervical cancer recurrence who received SBRT, the 2&#x02010;year local control and OS rates were 65% and 43%, respectively.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0190" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>190</sup>
</xref> Notably, smaller tumor volumes (&#x0003c;30 cm<sup>3</sup>) were associated with significantly improved 2&#x02010;year OS (89% vs. 12%; <italic toggle="yes">p</italic>&#x000a0;&#x0003c;&#x000a0;.001) and local progression&#x02010;free survival (85% vs. 0%; <italic toggle="yes">p</italic>&#x000a0;=&#x000a0;0.020). However, severe side effects&#x02014;including rectovaginal fistulas&#x02014;occurred in approximately 13% of patients, reinforcing the necessity of cautious patient selection and meticulous dose planning.</p></sec><sec id="caac70016-sec-0280"><title>Breast cancer</title><p>Breast reRT is generally indicated for patients with isolated locoregional recurrences, especially those with a long disease&#x02010;free interval. For well selected cases of in&#x02010;breast recurrences, repeat breast&#x02010;conserving surgery followed by partial breast reRT can be proposed as an alternative to mastectomy, offering comparable oncologic outcomes.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0056" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>56</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="caac70016-bib-0059" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>59</sup>
</xref>
</p><p>Key organs at risk in breast reRT include the heart, lungs, and brachial plexus, depending on the tumor location. Major side effects can include not only functional impairments but also aesthetic alterations, which may profoundly affect quality of life. For instance, cosmetic outcomes have been reported as fair&#x02010;to&#x02010;poor in up to 26.7% of cases when patients were treated with IMRT.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0191" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>191</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="caac70016-bib-0192" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>192</sup>
</xref>&#x000a0;The psychosocial effects of these changes, such as visible skin discoloration or scarring, underscore the importance of considering patient preferences and expectations in treatment planning.</p><p>Typical candidates for second breast&#x02010;conserving surgery and partial breast reRT include individuals aged 50 years and older who have unifocal recurrent tumors of &#x0003c;3 cm, no other high&#x02010;risk features (such as dermal or chest wall involvement or regional nodal disease), and a disease&#x02010;free interval of at least 3 years&#x02014;preferably 5 years&#x02014;since the initial RT course.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0193" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>193</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="caac70016-bib-0194" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>194</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="caac70016-bib-0195" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>195</sup>
</xref> The TAM score (combining the <italic toggle="yes">time interval</italic> with <italic toggle="yes">accelerated partial breast irradiation</italic>) eligibility criteria plus <italic toggle="yes">molecular subtype</italic>, is an effective tool in identifying suitable candidates for second breast&#x02010;conserving therapy.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0032" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>32</sup>
</xref> Importantly, both the radiation oncologist and the surgeon have to carefully estimate the ability to achieve acceptable cosmesis outcomes based on residual effects of the former RT, combined with an evaluation of preoperative and estimated postoperative volume and shape of the breast, which depend on tumor size and site related to the breast volume.</p><p>Accurate target localization for partial breast reRT in isolated in&#x02010;breast tumor recurrence requires a multidisciplinary approach. Breast MRI is essential for detailed visualization of the recurrent tumor and excluding multifocal disease, whereas <sup>18</sup>F&#x02010;FDG PET plays a key role in assessing both potential nodal involvement and distant metastasis and possibly can assist in target volume delineation.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0196" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>196</sup>
</xref>
</p><p>For effective partial breast reRT, careful target volume delineation is important to optimize tumor control while minimizing radiation exposure to healthy tissues. Surgical clip placement within the surgical cavity to mark the tumor bed during repeat breast&#x02010;conserving surgery facilitates accurate localization for reRT planning. A 2&#x02010;cm expansion around the primary tumor bed is recommended to encompass potential microscopic disease spread (Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="caac70016-fig-0014" ref-type="fig">14</xref>) within the confines of the breast tissue.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0197" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>197</sup>
</xref>
</p><fig position="float" fig-type="FIGURE" id="caac70016-fig-0014"><label>FIGURE 14</label><caption><p>Partial breast irradiation in the context of reRT. This is a treatment&#x02010;planning CT scan for partial breast irradiation in the setting of reRT for breast cancer. The magenta contour outlines the breast; surgical clips, marked in yellow, aid in identifying the primary tumor bed location. The primary tumor bed, shown in red, represents the original recurrence site. The CTV, generated as 2 cm minus the tumor&#x02010;free margin around the primary tumor bed, is displayed in light blue, covering the reRT area while sparing surrounding healthy tissue. CT indicates computed tomography; CTV, clinical target volume; reRT, reirradiation.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="CAAC-75-630-g013" position="anchor" id="jats-graphic-27"/></fig><p>By using brachytherapy for partial breast irradiation, a 10&#x02010;year local recurrence rate of 7.2% and favorable cosmetic and functional outcomes were obtained.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0198" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>198</sup>
</xref> Moreover, photon&#x02010;based partial breast reRT was shown to yield a 5&#x02010;year recurrence rate of 5%, with breast conservation achieved in 90% of patients.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0197" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>197</sup>
</xref> Proton therapy offers additional potential for cases involving the chest wall or sensitive normal tissue, possibly enabling more sparing of previously irradiated tissues and thereby potentially improving cosmetic and functional outcomes for these anatomically complex cases. The dose&#x02010;modulation capabilities of proton therapy may be especially useful for nodal recurrences, particularly in areas close to the brachial plexus, by optimizing the balance between tumor control and possible side effects.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0122" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>122</sup>
</xref>
</p></sec></sec><sec id="caac70016-sec-0290"><title>CONCLUSION</title><p>When considering reRT, an individualized approach is key, guided by established guidelines, such as the ESTRO/EORTC consensus<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0001" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>1</sup>
</xref> and expert clinical consensus&#x02010;based guidelines.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0042" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>42</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="caac70016-bib-0043" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>43</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="caac70016-bib-0044" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>44</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="caac70016-bib-0045" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>45</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="caac70016-bib-0046" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>46</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="caac70016-bib-0047" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>47</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="caac70016-bib-0048" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>48</sup>
</xref> The decision to proceed with reRT must balance the patient's health, tumor characteristics, and cumulative radiation dose to ensure effective treatment while minimizing the risk of side effects.</p><p>Patient selection is critical and should include a thorough consideration of the patient's quality&#x02010;of&#x02010;life expectations as well as potential effects on their daily functioning. The patient's PS, life expectancy, and comorbidities must be carefully assessed while balancing short&#x02010;term and long&#x02010;term sequelae from previous treatments against the immediate potential benefits of reRT to maximize outcomes for the individual patient. Residual organ function and dysfunction, particularly in previously irradiated tissues, is a key consideration because most organs have a limited cumulative tolerance to radiation. Tumor&#x02010;related factors also play a major role, including tumor location. Isolated locoregional recurrences, as opposed to the simultaneous presence of metastatic disease, are often prioritized for reRT because a curative&#x02010;intent treatment may be reasonable.</p><p>Advanced imaging methods like PET/CT and MRI help to accurately delineate the tumor and differentiate between true recurrences and treatment&#x02010;related changes, such as pseudoprogression. The aggressiveness of the recurrence, the time interval since the previous RT course, and prior tumor response to RT help inform the decision to pursue reRT. A thorough review of all prior radiation treatments is important to assess the cumulative dose delivered to the healthy tissues. This assessment helps to determine whether reRT can be proposed as an option with acceptable risks for side effects. Standard practice often involves summing the cumulative dose to evaluate total radiation doses received by the patient, which helps to determine the dose that may be safely delivered. Advanced RT techniques are typically used in reRT to achieve highly conformal delivery of tumoricidal doses while sparing healthy tissues. However, the choice of the technique also depends on the tumor's size, location, and proximity to surrounding healthy tissues. Systemic therapies, including chemotherapy or immunotherapy, may be incorporated to enhance the treatment effect, but this must be done with caution given the increased cumulative risk of side effects, especially in previously irradiated areas.</p><p>Tissue tolerance to reRT is a key consideration because healthy tissues may not fully recover from the first radiation course. Generally, the longer the interval since the initial radiation, the greater the potential for tissue recovery; however, the extent of recovery varies significantly between organs, with some tissues demonstrating a higher capacity for regeneration than others. In some tissues, such as the spinal cord and neural structures, a <italic toggle="yes">recovery factor</italic> is observed, lowering the risk of side effects over time. However, this phenomenon is not observed uniformly across all organs, and cumulative dose constraints remain a primary guide in reRT planning.</p><p>Patient&#x02010;centered considerations, including functional, aesthetic, and psychosocial issues, also must be integrated into the planning process. In some patients, reRT may be pursued with curative intent; whereas, in others, it may be palliative, aimed at controlling symptoms and improving quality and possibly duration of life. Patients must be fully informed about the actual goals, risks, and benefits, including potential uncertainties about side&#x02010;effect rates. These should be incorporated into a robust informed&#x02010;consent and decision&#x02010;making framework. Ultimately, the decision to proceed with reRT should be made using an interdisciplinary and multidisciplinary, collaborative approach, incorporating patient preferences, all oncologic treatment options, and clinical judgment to achieve the best possible outcome. This vision is strongly supported by the recent establishment of ReCOG (the Reirradiation Collaborative Group), which aims to address current knowledge gaps, promote standardization in reporting and dose&#x02010;accumulation practices, and facilitate international data&#x02010;pooling efforts to improve decision making in reRT.<xref rid="caac70016-bib-0199" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>199</sup>
</xref>
</p></sec><sec sec-type="COI-statement" id="caac70016-sec-0310"><title>CONFLICT OF INTEREST STATEMENT</title><p>Dr Kevin Chua reports support for professional activities from AstraZeneca, F. Hoffmann&#x02010;La Roche, Suzhou Liangyihui Network Technology, and Varian Medical Systems; and travel support from AstraZeneca outside the submitted work. Prof. Dorota Gabrys reports support for professional activities from Seimens Healthineers International AG outside the submitted work. Prof. Maximillian Niyazi reports grants/contracts from Brainlab Inc. and support for other professional activities from AstraZeneca AB outside the submitted work. Dr Kelly Paradis reports support for professional activities from Varian Medical Systems outside the submitted work. Dr Nicole C. Schmitt reports grants/contracts from Astex Pharmaceuticals; and personal/consulting fees from Checkpoint Surgical Inc., GeoVax, Johnson &#x00026; Johnson, Regeneron, Sensorion, and Synergy Research Inc. outside the submitted work. Prof. Sue S. Yom reports grants/contracts from Bristol Myers Squibb Company, Johnson &#x00026; Johnson Health Care Systems Inc., Merck, and Nanobiotix outside the submitted work. Prof. Nicholaus Andratschke reports grants/contracts from ViewRay Inc. outside the submitted work. The remaining authors disclosed no conflicts of interest.</p></sec></body><back><ack id="caac70016-sec-0300"><title>ACKNOWLEDGMENTS</title><p>We are grateful to Prof. Laura Rozenblum, Associate Professor in Nuclear Medicine at Piti&#x000e9;&#x02010;Salp&#x000ea;tri&#x000e8;re University Hospital, for her insightful comments. We also sincerely thank Dr Thomas Fr&#x000e9;d&#x000e9;ric Moreau, Radiation Oncologist, and Mr Alexandre Roque, Medical Physicist, for their valuable assistance in developing the dosimetric figures. We are grateful to Laura Kenney for her meticulous English editing of the article. Dr Jonas Willmann is supported by the Swiss National Science Foundation (Grant P500PM_203194). Dr Ane L. Appelt acknowledges Cancer Research UK funding for the Leeds Radiotherapy Research Centre of Excellence (Grant RRCOER&#x02010;Jun24/100004). No funding was received for this work.</p></ack><ref-list content-type="cited-references" id="caac70016-bibl-0001"><title>REFERENCES</title><ref id="caac70016-bib-0001"><label>1</label><mixed-citation publication-type="journal" id="caac70016-cit-0001">
